{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/dao.space.index.md docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md'","aggregated_output":"# dao.space.index — The Transactor-Side Indexing Library\n\nStatus: implemented. The mechanism (owner-built, content-addressed B-Tree\nsegments) shipped 2026-07-10 and runs today on `dao.data.btree`; this document\nrecords why the library exists, what it owns, its public surface, and the\nboundary between it and the query library. The executable contract is\n`test/dao/space/index_test.cljc`.\n\n**Related documents:**\n- `docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md` — the write path that calls\n  `publish-index!`; the local-stream wiring requirement lives there and here\n- `docs/design/dao.space.query.md` — the reader-side consumer of the\n  realization this library owns\n- `docs/design/dao.space.md` — the tuple space; *Three Boundaries* maps\n  Transactor/Storage/Query onto streams\n- `docs/design/dao.jing.md` — the storage boundary; publication through an\n  explicit intake pool\n- `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` — the distributed backend the published blobs\n  can be stored into\n- `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` — the cross-platform tree and its storage\n  adapter\n- `docs/datomic.md` — the architecture the analogy below leans on\n\n## Why a separate library\n\nIn Datomic, the **transactor** builds the covered indexes and saves them to\nstorage; **peers** pull segments and answer queries. datom.world decentralizes\nthe transactor: every agent appending to its own `dao.stream` is its own\ntransactor (`dao.space.md`, *Three Boundaries*) — so every agent also owns the\ntransactor's other duty, **indexing its own datoms**. That duty needs a\nlibrary the same way querying does:\n\n- `dao.space.index` — what a stream owner runs: snapshot the local stream,\n  build the covered indexes, and enqueue them as immutable content-addressed\n  segments plus a manifest through a DaoJing intake stream. The write-side\n  counterpart of the pair.\n- `dao.space.query` — the embeddable Peer: open bounded source coordinates,\n  match, run Datalog, and pull. The transportable read coordinate belongs to\n  `dao.space.index`; query's `open-published!` opens it, consuming only its\n  logical d5 rows and covered sets.\n\nThe boundary is strict: **query never writes; index owns the realization both\nsides share.** It is the same move Datomic makes between transactor and peer —\none party materializes, many parties consume — except here \"the transactor\"\nis not a process but a library duty every agent carries.\n\nCovered indexes are physical access paths within the DaoSpace tuple-space\npoint. They change lookup cost, not tuple meaning. Replacing or supplementing\nthem with explicitly requested positional indexes would still be DaoSpace as\nlong as the logical operation remains exact associative tuple matching.\n\n## What the library owns\n\nOne namespace, `src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc`. Everything below is the index\n*realization*: the shared vocabulary a builder and a reader must agree on.\n\n- **Sort orders** — `eavt-cmp` / `aevt-cmp` / `avet-cmp` / `vaet-cmp` over\n  heterogeneous values (`compare-vals`: type-ranked, nil-first). The\n  comparators also serve query-only db-values, whose ids may be caller-chosen;\n  persisted local d5 datoms are stricter: non-negative integer e/t, integer\n  m, and namespaced keyword a. The library also owns the datom slot accessors\n  (`datom-e/a/v/t/m`) they read through. Covered indexes contain canonical\n  local d5 datoms only. Source scope belongs to the query interpreter and is\n  not appended to an indexed tuple.\n- **The in-memory index value** — `index-datoms` builds\n  `{:eavt :aevt :avet :vaet}` `dao.data.btree` sorted sets. `subseq-from`\n  delegates to `dao.data.btree/slice`: a log-n descent that, on a restored\n  tree, loads only the seek path plus the matching range. The implementation\n  is the same on JVM, ClojureScript, and ClojureDart.\n- **The snapshot** — `snapshot-datoms` reads an agent-local stream in full\n  through the dao.stream reader surface: mint an `:oldest` cursor, then\n  `next` to the tail. The local stream must be on a complete-retention\n  transport — a handle created by `dao.stream.memory-log/create!`; its\n  declared complete retention is why a fresh `:oldest` cursor is the origin,\n  so the read starts at the logical sequence's beginning and `gap` cannot\n  occur. `stream/reader?`/`stream/writer?` check *surfaces*, not retention —\n  they do not establish the completeness this read depends on. A stream\n  element is either one canonical datom vector `[e a v t m]` or one atomic\n  transaction record `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}`;\n  transaction records are validated and flattened into their datoms. Each\n  element is validated and flattened as it is read, so the first defect in\n  stream order is the one reported and no read happens past it. Every result\n  is validated with `stream/validate-outcome` before it is interpreted — a\n  malformed result (say, an `ok` carrying no cursor) throws instead of\n  recurring on a nil cursor. `blocked` (an open stream caught up) and `end`\n  (a closed one fully read) finish the snapshot at the tail; a well-formed\n  outcome outside `ok`/`blocked`/`end` means the handle is not the\n  complete-retention transport the composition owes, and throws before\n  anything is emitted.\n- **The persisted node-blob format, both directions** — a\n  `dao.data.btree/IStorage` adapter over a DaoJing content-store handle\n  (`dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage`): nodes store as plain-EDN\n  content-addressed segment blobs (leaf `{:keys [...]}`, branch\n  `{:level n :keys [...] :addresses [...]}`), addresses minted by\n  `dao.jing/materialize!` — Merkle by construction, since `dao.data.btree`\n  stores children before parents. `read-manifest` validates a manifest and its\n  content address; `read-datoms` walks the EAVT node graph eagerly using only\n  `jing/get`; `restored-indexes` re-attaches a published manifest's trees\n  lazily on every platform.\n- **The transportable read coordinate** — `published-index` constructs a\n  serializable exact-bounded coordinate from a DaoJing coordinate and an\n  immutable manifest address. Opening it is the reader's move:\n  `dao.space.query`'s `open-published!` validates the coordinate, opens the\n  store, fetches only the manifest, re-attaches the covered sets lazily\n  (restored-indexes), and defers the EAVT rows behind a delay the reader\n  owns; `read-datoms` here walks those rows eagerly. Physical B-tree nodes\n  never cross the boundary.\n- **The transactor entry point** — `publish-index!`: snapshot the stream,\n  build the four covered indexes, append the node blobs and the manifest to\n  one intake stream selected from an explicit pool. DaoJing itself is never\n  mutated directly.\n\n## The manifest\n\nThe published manifest is exactly\n\n```clojure\n{:indexes {:eavt <segment-address-or-nil>\n           :aevt <segment-address-or-nil>\n           :avet <segment-address-or-nil>\n           :vaet <segment-address-or-nil>}\n :count n\n :branching-factor n}\n```\n\nwith no source stream, no pool, no epoch, and no self-address. `read-manifest`\naccepts only this shape: the four index keys, a non-negative integer `:count`,\nan integer `:branching-factor` of at least two, index addresses all nil when\nthe count is zero and all `:segment/sha256-...` addresses otherwise, and the\nmanifest's content address matching the requested address. The manifest\naddress is derived from the manifest alone and never depends on which intake\nstream carried it. `:count` is the cardinality of the covered index set, not\nthe number of duplicate occurrences in the source stream; all four restored\ntrees therefore report the same O(1) count they actually contain.\n\n## Public surface\n\n```clojure\n(require '[dao.space.index :as index])\n\n;; the transactor's move (JVM, ClojureScript, and ClojureDart)\n(index/publish-index! local-stream intake-pool)\n(index/publish-index! local-stream intake-pool\n                      {:branching-factor 512})    ; max keys per node,\n                                                  ; Datomic-style fat segments\n(index/publish-index! local-stream intake-pool\n                      {:select-stream f})         ; f receives the pool and\n                                                  ; returns one intake stream\n;; => {:manifest-address :segment/sha256-… :manifest {:indexes {...} :count n :branching-factor n}}\n\n;; the snapshot (reads and validates a local stream)\n(index/snapshot-datoms local-stream)              ; => flattened datom seq\n\n;; the payload vocabulary's public, seq-level spelling\n(index/datoms-from-elements elements)             ; => flattened datom vector\n\n;; the format's readers (every platform)\n(index/published-index {:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file :path path}\n                       manifest-address)                  ; transportable read\n                                                          ; coordinate — open it\n                                                          ; with query's\n                                                          ; open-published!\n(index/read-manifest content-store manifest-address)     ; validated manifest\n(index/read-datoms content-store manifest-address)       ; eager EAVT walk\n(index/walk-index-datoms content-store segment-address)  ; eager node-graph walk\n(index/restored-indexes content-store manifest)          ; lazy B-tree re-attach\n\n;; the shared vocabulary\n(index/index-datoms datoms)                     ; {:eavt :aevt :avet :vaet} trees\n(index/subseq-from sorted-set index/eavt-cmp sentinel)\n(index/compare-vals a b)\n(index/eavt-cmp) (index/aevt-cmp) (index/avet-cmp) (index/vaet-cmp)\n(index/datom-e d) (index/datom-a d) (index/datom-v d)\n(index/datom-t d) (index/datom-m d)\n```\n\n**`datoms-from-elements` is the payload vocabulary's public spelling.** A\nlocal-stream element is one canonical d5 datom vector or one atomic\n`{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}` record; `datoms-from-elements`\nflattens a seq of them, validating each element through the same private\nper-element rule (`element-datoms`) that rejects malformed datoms, malformed\ntransaction records, and non-elements with distinct diagnostics. It has no\n`src` caller, by design — do not \"clean it up\": `snapshot-datoms` deliberately\nuses the per-element form inside its read loop rather than this seq-level one,\nso each element is validated and flattened as it is read (the first defect in\nstream order is the one reported, and no read happens past it), and both\nspellings go through the same `element-datoms`.\n\n`publish-index!` semantics worth pinning:\n\n- **Builds into a temporary recording content handle.** The four indexes are\n  stored through `dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage` into a build-time\n  in-memory handle whose put records each unique blob on first insertion\n  (deduplicating equal blobs as `:present`), so the recorded order is exactly\n  the children-before-parent store-tree traversal.\n- **Exactly one intake stream.** `publish-index!` selects one stream from the\n  explicit pool — the first, or the stream returned by `:select-stream`, which\n  must be a member of the pool. It appends every unique node blob in recorded\n  order, then the manifest last.\n- **Append success means enqueued, not materialized.** Success acknowledges\n  that every payload was appended to the selected intake stream. A DaoJing\n  observer must still read the stream for the payloads to land in content\n  storage.\n- **Partial immutable prefix is retry-safe.** The manifest is always appended\n  last, so a full intake stream can only have left node blobs; the next\n  publish re-emits them and materialization deduplicates. No partial manifest\n  is ever observable.\n- **No direct DaoJing mutation.** Publication writes streams, never a content\n  store; DaoJing observes.\n- **Idempotent on unchanged data** — content addressing yields the same\n  segment keys, so the same manifest address; republishing costs writes that\n  are no-ops at materialization.\n- **An empty stream publishes nil index addresses** —\n  `{:indexes {:eavt nil …}}` reads back as no datoms, not an error (a walk of\n  nil ⇒ ()).\n- **Publication changes representation, not visibility** — append has already\n  written the datoms to the local stream. Publication replaces no root; it\n  adds a persisted covered-index manifest over the same datoms.\n\n## The agent-transactor loop\n\nThe write path runs through `dao.space.transactor`'s value — a plain map\ncreated by `transactor/create!` over a spec, not a stream and not a\ndescriptor (see `dao.space.transactor.md`, and `dao.space.md`, *The Write\nPath*). Its spec is `{:local-stream s :intake-pool [...] optional :name}`;\nit owns neither stream lifecycle — the local stream and intake pool are\nsupplied, never created, registered, or closed:\n\n```clojure\n(require '[dao.stream.memory-log :as memory-log]\n         '[dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]\n         '[dao.space.transactor :as transactor])\n\n(def local (:dao.stream/handle                        ; the agent's own log, a\n            (memory-log/create!                       ; complete-retention\n              {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/memory-log})))  ; memory-log\n(def intake-pool [(:dao.stream/handle\n                    (ringbuffer/create!\n                      {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ringbuffer\n                       :dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity 4096}))])\n\n(def log (transactor/create! {:local-stream local\n                              :intake-pool intake-pool\n                              :name \"worker-7\"}))     ; one wrapper per local stream\n\n(transactor/append! log {:db/id id :work/claims task}) ; 1. deposit — one atomic\n;; ... more appends ...                                ;    transaction record\n(transactor/publish! log)                              ; 2. snapshot, build, enqueue\n```\n\nThe value's `append!` / `transact!` write exactly one atomic transaction\nrecord to the local stream per call, so no reader observes a torn transaction.\nOn `create!` it reads the retained history from its origin and derives the\nnext `t` (0 for an empty history, else 1 + the maximum datom t); the local\nstream must therefore be on a complete-retention transport —\n`dao.stream.memory-log/create!`. `create!` validates reader/writer\n*surfaces*, never retention: a `stream/reader?`/`stream/writer?` check does\nnot establish complete retention, and supplying an evicting transport is a\nhost-assembly defect (detectable, deliberately not checked — see\n`dao.space.transactor.md`, T18). One wrapper per local stream is a hard\nsingle-writer invariant. Calls through one wrapper serialize timestamp\nallocation and append on shared-memory hosts; two wrappers over the same\nstream still each derive the same `t` and write colliding records, which\ncannot be silently coordinated without shared mutable state. `publish!` delegates explicitly to\n`index/publish-index!`, passing the wrapper's local stream, intake pool, and\nopts, and returning its `{:manifest-address ... :manifest ...}`.\n\nTwo lifecycle facts are deliberate:\n\n- Publication is an acceleration, never a semantic change — `q`/`match`\n  answer identically before and after (pinned by\n  `publish-index-snapshot-reads-local-stream-and-reads-back` and the\n  observer-materialization parity tests).\n- `query/open-published!` opens the published coordinate lazily (manifest\n  fetch plus restored sets) and defers the EAVT rows behind `query/rows`;\n  query consumes the restored sets directly for selective current reads,\n  forces the rows for rest-pattern and 4+-slot scans, and walks the restored\n  tree for unselective 3-fixed scans. Query does not dispatch on manifests,\n  pools, or B-tree segments. Publishing changes access cost, never\n  the datoms.\n\n## Dependency picture\n\n```\ndao.space.transactor  ──►  dao.space.index  ◄──  dao.space.query\n   (write path:          (realization:          (the Peer:\n    append!/transact!      B-tree values,          open published coordinate,\n    write transaction      publish-index!,         match, q, pull —\n    records; publish!      sort orders,            reads relations and views\n    delegates here)        node blobs)             or published manifests)\n                                │\n                                ▼\n                          dao.data.btree ◄── dao.data.btree.storage\n                          (the tree)            (IStorage over a content handle)\n                                │\n                                ▼\n                          dao.jing (content-store handle)\n```\n\n- `dao.space.query` requires `dao.space.index` for manifest reading and index\n  traversal. It does not define or persist a second index format.\n- `dao.space.transactor` requires `dao.space.index` for snapshotting,\n  validation, and publication. The write path does not depend on the Datalog\n  engine.\n- `dao.space.index` requires `dao.data.btree`,\n  `dao.data.btree.storage`, and `dao.jing`; it never requires\n  `dao.space.query`. No cycle is possible: realization below, interpretation\n  above.\n\nStorage stays dumb throughout (Decision 1 of `dao.space.query.md`, no new\nstorage protocol): everything\nhere is built from `dao.jing/materialize!` and `dao.jing/get` against strict\nsegment addresses. Storage never knows the segment blobs form an index, and\nthere is no mutable root for it to maintain.\n\n## Platform status\n\nBuild, the eager published read (`read-datoms`), lazy lower-level\nrestoration, and range slicing are all\n**cross-platform**. `dao.data.btree` and `dao.data.btree.storage` are `.cljc`\nimplementations shared by JVM, ClojureScript, and ClojureDart.\n\nThe eager published path uses `walk-index-datoms`, which understands the plain EDN node\nblobs using only `jing/get`. The separate lazy API uses\n`dao.data.btree/restore-tree` through\n`dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage`; traversal faults only the required nodes.\nThe manifest's `:count` and `:branching-factor` are threaded through\n`restore-tree` deliberately: count keeps O(1) `count` on restored trees\nwithout faulting the graph, and the branching factor reaches every restored\nnode so mutation splits at the published thresholds. Tests in\n`test/dao/space/index_test.cljc` and `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc` pin\nboth contracts, including lazy point-lookup fetch counts for the\nlower-level restored tree and, in `query_test`, coordinate transport and\neager logical d5 reads through the opened published value.\n\n## Open items\n\n- **Segment GC** — superseded index segments accumulate forever.\n- **Published indexed snapshots and K-way merge** — queries currently consume\n  each published manifest through one opened coordinate at a time.\n- **Incremental indexing** — the natural next increment for long-lived agent\n  transactors: today an owner republishes wholesale from the full datom seq. A\n  future builder could retain the previous manifest, insert only the appended\n  datoms into restored B-trees, and store their changed paths. Nothing in the\n  node or manifest format requires that strategy; the current implementation\n  always performs a full rebuild. The design for this is\n  [`dao.space.index.as-observer.md`](./dao.space.index.as-observer.md): this\n  library driven batch by batch through `dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream`\n  over any medium, with `publish-index!` kept as the stateless special case.\n- **Async hydration** — remote reads over async backends use the hydration\n  adapter (`dao.data.btree.storage/hydration-storage`, `hydrate!`); the async\n  variants (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`) are deferred until an async\n  DaoJing backend exists.\n# dao.space.transactor — The Agent-Side Transactor\n\nStatus: implemented on `dao.stream` (migrated 2026-09-09; the v1\n`:transactor` DaoStream type it replaces is deleted). This document is the\npermanent record of the transactor's contract: what a transactor is, the\ninvariants its tests pin, where durability lives, and the one wiring\nrequirement a host composition must honour. The executable contract is\n`test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc`.\n\n**Related documents:**\n- `docs/design/dao.space.md` — the tuple space; *Three Boundaries* and\n  *The Write Path* place the transactor in the medium\n- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — `publish!` delegates here from the\n  transactor; the snapshot reads this namespace's local stream\n- `docs/design/dao.space.schema.md` — the validating wrapper that owns a\n  transactor value\n- `docs/design/dao.jing.md` — the storage boundary publication targets\n- `docs/design/dao.stream.md` and `docs/design/dao.stream.md` — the\n  contract this namespace consumes\n\n## A transactor is a value, not a stream\n\nA transactor is an **interpreter over a stream, not a stream**\n(`dao.stream.md`, *Composition*): it reads via the local stream's reader\nsurface, appends through its writer surface, and needs no support from the\nstream contract for either. The v1 `:transactor` DaoStream type — a\nregistered dispatch record carrying two live handles — could never cross the\ndescriptor boundary v2 requires, so it was never a descriptor; its options\nmap is now the constructor's plain **spec**:\n\n```clojure\n(require '[dao.stream.memory-log :as memory-log]\n         '[dao.space.transactor :as transactor])\n\n(def local (:dao.stream/handle\n            (memory-log/create! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/memory-log})))\n\n(def log (transactor/create! {:local-stream local   ; reader+writer surface;\n                              :intake-pool [intake] ; supplied, never owned\n                              :name \"worker-7\"}))   ; optional, diagnostic\n\n(transactor/append!   log {:db/id 1 :work/claims \"task\"})\n(transactor/transact! log [{:db/id 1 :work/claims \"task\"} …])\n(transactor/publish!  log opts)   ; → {:manifest-address … :manifest …}\n(transactor/close!    log)        ; → {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}\n;; reads: use `local` — it is the caller's handle and always was\n```\n\nThere is no registry and no `open!` dispatch: `create!` is a plain function,\nfollowing `query/open-published!`. A transactor implements no dao.stream\nprotocol at all — it is not on a stream, so it has no identity to project.\n`next` was pure delegation on a handle the caller still holds, and `closed?`\nis *Explicitly Absent* in v2 because \"a predicate answer is stale the moment\nit returns; operation results are authoritative\" — the replacement is in the\nvocabulary: `append!`/`transact!` on a closed transactor answer\n`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` as data.\n\n`create!` returns **the value, or throws** — its failure modes are a\nmalformed spec and a malformed retained history, both of which a caller can\nonly abort on. It validates **surfaces, never retention**:\n`stream/reader?`/`stream/writer?` (T16's pool check is the same surface\nrule), and a reader/writer surface check does not establish retention.\n\n## The invariants\n\nPinned by `test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc` unless noted:\n\n| # | Invariant |\n|---|---|\n| T1 | Every `append!`/`transact!` writes exactly ONE atomic transaction record `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}` through exactly one local append, so no reader observes a torn transaction |\n| T2 | `t` comes from a per-value watermark, derived on `create!` as 0 for an empty history else 1 + max datom `t` |\n| T3 | A caller-supplied `:next-t` is rejected |\n| T4a | A malformed retained history fails the create |\n| T5 | The watermark advances **iff** the local append answered `:dao.stream/ok`; a failed or thrown append leaves the same `t` retryable |\n| T6 | Single-writer: two values over one local stream derive the same `t` and write colliding records; a documented hazard, not coordinated |\n| T7 | Close is per value: it rejects further writes and neither closes nor erases the local stream or the intake pool |\n| T8 | Close linearizes after an in-flight append |\n| T9 | Creating a transactor creates, registers, or closes nothing |\n| T10 | Entity maps require `:db/id`; an explicit datom `t` is rejected; `[e a v]`/`[e a v nil m]` pad and validate |\n| T11 | `publish!` passes the local stream, pool and opts to `index/publish-index!` and returns its result |\n| T15 | An ok receipt carries the outcome, the allocated `t`, and the datoms — `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.space/t t :dao.space/datoms ds}` |\n| T16 | Intake pool members are validated with v2 `stream/writer?`; a non-empty collection is required |\n| T18 | The local stream is on a transport declaring complete retention (below) |\n| T19 | `dao.space.schema` installs its next state only when the transactor answered ok (pinned in `schema_test`) |\n| T20 | Retired 2026-09-09 under schema's plan; schema returns the transactor's receipt unchanged and adopts its closed-precedence rule |\n\n## Operational outcomes are data; argument defects throw\n\nThe write path validates the local append's answer with\n`stream/valid-outcome?` before interpreting it, and answers:\n\n| Case | v2 behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| local append ok | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.space/t t :dao.space/datoms ds}`, watermark advances |\n| local append `full`/`closed`/`invalid-value`/`transport-error` | returned as data, watermark **unchanged**, same `t` still pending |\n| local append answers a non-outcome | folded to `:dao.stream/transport-error` with `:dao.stream/answer` retained |\n| transactor itself closed | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` |\n| malformed entity map / explicit datom `t` / empty `tx-data` / zero datoms | **still throws** — defects in the caller's own argument, detected before any stream is touched |\n| local append throws | propagates, watermark unchanged |\n\n`full` means \"not yet, retry\", so throwing on it was always wrong; the retry\ncontract is now stronger, not weaker: *the watermark advances if and only if\nthe local append answered `:dao.stream/ok`*, so retrying the identical call\nre-attempts the identical `t`. On a `memory-log` local stream the only\nreachable non-ok outcome is `closed`, since the transport excludes `full`,\n`invalid-value` and `transport-error` — but `append!`/`transact!` are library\nfunctions over whatever handle they are given, which is why the `full` branch\nis pinned by a writer double rather than left untested.\n\n## T18: the local-log wiring requirement\n\n> The host composition supplies `dao.space` a local handle created by\n> `dao.stream.memory-log/create!`. Its declared complete retention makes\n> fresh `:oldest` cursors true origin cursors for `derive-next-t` and\n> `publish-index!`; supplying an evicting transport is a host-assembly defect.\n\nA **declaration, not a check**. `create!` does not and must not verify\nretention, although the misassembly is detectable at least twice over:\n`stream/descriptor` exposes `:dao.stream/type`, so a type check would catch a\nring buffer today, and the contract's *Complete history* names the kept\norigin cursor as a detection mechanism in its own right. Neither is done,\non purpose:\n\n- **A type check would couple `dao.space` to `memory-log` by name** and\n  reject a future correct transport — a durable append-only log, a spilling\n  log — that declares the same complete retention. That is precisely what\n  \"declared, never interrogated\" exists to prevent, and why `dao.stream.md`\n  lists a retention predicate as *Explicitly Absent*.\n- **A kept origin cursor buys nothing here.** On a complete-retention\n  transport a fresh `:oldest` *is* the origin, and the transactor does not\n  create the local stream, so it could not mint at origin even if it wanted\n  to. The mechanism is for consumers on transports that can evict.\n\nSo the residual risk is real and accepted deliberately: a wrongly wired\nevicting transport's surviving suffix would be read and reported as the\nwhole history, silently. A `stream/reader?`/`stream/writer?` surface check\ndoes not establish retention — the defence is this requirement being written\nwhere someone wiring a composition will read it: here, in\n`dao.space.index.md` (*The snapshot* / *The agent-transactor loop*), in\n`dao.space.md` (*The Write Path*), and in ADR 0003's amendment.\n\n## Where durability lives\n\nThe local stream is the **authoritative record for the logical stream's\nlifetime** — the watermark and every published index are derived from it —\nand it is **not durable**: `memory-log` is process-lifetime, and a restarted\nprocess sees a new, empty logical stream with a new identity. That is the\ncorrect shape, not a shortfall:\n\n- **Stage 1, append** — the write lands in the agent's local stream and no\n  storage handle is touched.\n- **Stage 2, publish** — the covered indexes and manifest are appended to an\n  intake stream and a DaoJing observer materializes them into content\n  storage.\n\n**The durable record is what publication puts in `dao.jing`.** Un-published\nwrites are not durable, and never were — v1's local streams were in-memory\nring buffers, so this was equally true before the migration; the design\nmerely claimed otherwise. The pipeline mirrors Datomic's memory-index →\ndisk-index, and a memory index is not durable either. A durable *stream*\ntransport is not the answer and should not be proposed as the fix: it would\nput a second durable record beside the content store, which is exactly the\n`dao.stream`/`dao.jing` unification `dao.space.query.md`'s *Decisions*\nalready ruled out.\n\n## Open items\n\n- **The O(history) watermark replay.** `create!` derives `t` by reading the\n  whole retained history (`index/snapshot-datoms`), and `publish!` rebuilds\n  the indexes from the origin — both correct today *because the transport\n  cannot evict*. The eventual relief keeps one truth — the log carries its\n  own checkpoint, from which the watermark and incremental indexes resume —\n  rather than adding a second stored counter that can disagree with the\n  record. Not designed here.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# dao.space.index as a dao.stream Observer\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design note, 2026-09-13. `dao.space.index` is already the dao.stream\n     4\tobserver on the dao.space side: `snapshot-datoms` + `publish-index!` is one\n     5\tobserver run — attach at `:oldest`, fold to `blocked`, publish, keep nothing.\n     6\tThis note makes it a *stateful* observer, driven by\n     7\t`dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` over *any* `dao.stream` medium. No\n     8\tnew namespace: everything here is index realization, which `dao.space.index`\n     9\talready owns. The library specified here is payload-agnostic: it sees d5 rows\n    10\tand a supplied ref schema, and nothing below knows or cares what the rows\n    11\tmean or who else reads the medium. Subordinate to\n    12\t[`dao.space.md`](./dao.space.md) (write path, three boundaries),\n    13\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) (§Composition), and the observer\n    14\tcoordination already specified by `dao.stream.observer`. It\n    15\tcomposes with [`yin.vm.macro.md`](./yin.vm.macro.md) §4.2, whose \"commit\n    16\t`program-in` first for durable provenance\" becomes an instance of this\n    17\tnote.\n    18\t\n    19\t**Why this note exists** — a composition it enables, not a fact the index\n    20\tknows: attach a `yin.vm` evaluator and `dao.space.index` to the same medium,\n    21\tand the VM constructs CESK state while the index materializes covered\n    22\tindexes over the same batches, so `dao.space.query/q` answers Datalog over\n    23\tthe program being executed, as it grows. Neither observer is aware of the\n    24\tother; the stream is the only coupling, and the index's ignorance of what it\n    25\tindexes is as load-bearing as the VM's ignorance of being indexed. That\n    26\tinvariant belongs to the composition and is stated where compositions are\n    27\t(`datom.world.md`, `dao.space.md` §Three Boundaries); this document\n    28\tspecifies only the index side.\n    29\t\n    30\t---\n    31\t\n    32\t## 0. Decisions\n    33\t\n    34\t1. **dao.space observes; it does not have to be the writer.** Today\n    35\t   indexing is \"the writer's duty\" over the writer's own local stream\n    36\t   (`dao.space.md` §The Write Path). That stays true *as a composition*, but\n    37\t   the mechanism becomes an observer over a medium — any medium, whoever\n    38\t   writes it and whoever else reads it.\n    39\t2. **One coordination loop, one library.** `dao.space.index` is driven by\n    40\t   `dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` with its state in the `:consumer`\n    41\t   slot, exactly as an evaluator or the macro expander is. No second\n    42\t   observer machinery, and no new namespace: \"the dao.space observer\" is a\n    43\t   role `dao.space.index` plays when so driven, not a module.\n    44\t3. **The index is incremental and persistent.** Each observed batch is\n    45\t   folded into the four covered indexes as `dao.data.btree` values; the\n    46\t   trees are structurally shared across batches. `publish-index!`'s\n    47\t   full-rebuild-from-origin becomes a consequence of *starting* an observer\n    48\t   at `:oldest`, not the only way to build an index.\n    49\t4. **The observer never rewrites a datom and never allocates `t`.**\n    50\t   Transaction time is the writer's (the transactor's). The observer records\n    51\t   what it saw and *where* (its own batch ordinal) as facts *about* the\n    52\t   batch, in its own metadata namespace, never by editing rows.\n    53\t5. **Identity resolution is the observer's reading.** Tempids are\n    54\t   batch-local; the observer assigns durable ids per batch and records the\n    55\t   mapping as **resolution facts**. This is the one place cross-medium\n    56\t   identity can exist, because dao.space is the one observer that may read\n    57\t   every medium.\n    58\t6. **The index is payload-agnostic.** It interprets d5 shape and the\n    59\t   supplied ref schema, nothing else: it never evaluates, never inspects an\n    60\t   attribute's meaning, and never learns what another observer of the same\n    61\t   medium does with the rows. (This is what rules out the\n    62\t   \"CESK-in-the-index\" premise of `yin.vm-in-dao.space.md`: two interpreters\n    63\t   over one stream, not one interpreter with two faces.)\n    64\t\n    65\t---\n    66\t\n    67\t## 1. Today, and the gap\n    68\t\n    69\tAll of `dao.space` is on `dao.stream` (no v1 references remain). Its\n    70\tstream use has three shapes:\n    71\t\n    72\t| Piece | Shape today |\n    73\t|---|---|\n    74\t| `dao.space.transactor` | write-side interpreter over an agent's *own* local memory-log; wraps each transaction in one `{:dao.space/transaction {:t :datoms}}` record; derives `t` by scanning the whole retained history at `create!` |\n    75\t| `dao.space.index/publish-index!` | `snapshot-datoms` the local stream **in full from `:oldest`**, build four btrees from scratch, append node blobs then manifest to an intake stream |\n    76\t| `dao.space.query/snapshot` | the read side's one v2 interpreter: mint `:oldest`, loop `observe/step` into a relation, stop at `blocked`/`ended`/`gap`/`defect`; a **one-shot** |\n    77\t\n    78\tThree consequences follow, and they are the gap:\n    79\t\n    80\t- **Nothing indexes a medium dao.space did not write.** Any other writer's\n    81\t  medium — a program stream, an expander's log — carries datoms nobody\n    82\t  indexes; querying them means a full `snapshot` each time, which is\n    83\t  Datalog *as of* a call, never over a medium as it grows.\n    84\t- **No index is incremental.** Every publication rebuilds from the origin;\n    85\t  every `create!` replays history for the watermark. The transactor's own\n    86\t  *Open items* names this as O(history) and asks for \"one truth — the log\n    87\t  carries its own checkpoint\". An observer that holds a persistent index\n    88\t  *is* that checkpoint's natural owner.\n    89\t- **`snapshot` is not a session.** It mints a fresh cursor per call and\n    90\t  cannot be resumed batch by batch, so it cannot participate in a\n    91\t  `run-on-stream` round the way an evaluator does.\n    92\t\n    93\t**Already true.** `dao.space.index` is the consumer side of observation.\n    94\t`snapshot-datoms` + `publish-index!` *is* an observer run: attach at\n    95\t`:oldest`, fold everything to `blocked`, publish once, discard the state —\n    96\tthe degenerate case of `run-on-stream` with a consumer that is always ready\n    97\tand keeps nothing between calls. `datoms-from-elements` already accepts both\n    98\telement shapes (raw d5 rows and transaction records), `index-datoms` already\n    99\tbuilds the in-memory index, and `observe/step` already exists.\n   100\t\n   101\t**Not yet true**, and it is code, not wording:\n   102\t\n   103\t1. *Incremental fold.* `index-datoms` builds a whole index from a whole datom\n   104\t   vector; `publish-index!` rebuilds from the origin every time. Being\n   105\t   driven batch by batch needs `fold-batch : index-state batch →\n   106\t   index-state'` over persistent btree values (§2.2).\n   107\t2. *Tempid resolution across batches.* A transactor-written medium carries\n   108\t   resolved ids; a medium written batch by batch without a transactor\n   109\t   carries per-batch negative tempids that *repeat*. `dao.space.index` never\n   110\t   meets that case today because it\n   111\t   only indexes the writer's own resolved log. Observing a medium it did not\n   112\t   write requires the per-batch allocator and resolution facts (§3) — a\n   113\t   correctness requirement, not a framing one.\n   114\t3. *Bad input as data.* `snapshot-datoms` throws on the first malformed\n   115\t   element, which is right for a one-shot over your own log and\n   116\t   head-of-line-blocks a session over someone else's medium (§2.2 step 1).\n   117\t\n   118\t---\n   119\t\n   120\t## 2. The index consumer (`dao.space.index`)\n   121\t\n   122\t### 2.1 The index state\n   123\t\n   124\t```\n   125\tindex-state = {:indexes  {:eavt bt :aevt bt :avet bt :vaet bt}   ; dao.data.btree values, structurally shared across batches\n   126\t               :storage  <IStorage over a content handle>       ; the trees' storage; persists across publishes (§4.1)\n   127\t               :mode     :resolved | :unresolved                 ; fixed at construction (§3.1)\n   128\t               :ids      nil | {:next-eid n}                     ; allocator; present only in :unresolved mode\n   129\t               :batch    n                                       ; ordinal of the next batch, observer-local\n   130\t               :schema   {attr {:db/valueType :db.type/ref ...}} ; supplied; which attrs are refs\n   131\t               :publish  nil | {:intake writer :staged nil|payloads}   ; optional, §4\n   132\t               :defects  []}                                     ; malformed batches seen since last drain\n   133\t\n   134\t;; all in dao.space.index, beside index-datoms and publish-index!\n   135\tready?         (fn [x] (nil? (get-in x [:publish :staged])))\n   136\tload           index/fold-batch                                ; pure; §2.2\n   137\trun            index/flush-staged                              ; retries a staged publication; identity otherwise (§4)\n   138\tindex/publish! index-state → index-state'                      ; explicit: stage this state's trees, then flush (§4)\n   139\tindex/drain    (fn [x] [(assoc x :defects []) (:defects x)])\n   140\t```\n   141\t\n   142\tDriven as `(run-on-stream {:observer o :consumer index-state} ready?\n   143\tindex/fold-batch index/flush-staged)` over any `dao.stream` reader\n   144\thandle attached through `dao.stream.observer/attach`. The coordination\n   145\tinspects no field of the index state; it drives it as readily as a VM —\n   146\twhich is the point of decision 2. **Publication is an explicit composition\n   147\tstep**, not a policy inside the loop: the composition calls `publish!`\n   148\tbetween rounds (after every *n* rounds, on `blocked`, on a timer — its\n   149\tchoice), and only the *retry* of a staged publication that answered `full`\n   150\tlives inside `run`, so that `ready?` stays false until the intake accepted\n   151\tit. Calling `publish!` while a publication is still staged is a caller\n   152\terror (the composition checks `ready?` first); the staged payload list is\n   153\tnever replaced, so at-most-once per manifest holds.\n   154\t\n   155\t### 2.2 `fold-batch`\n   156\t\n   157\tOne batch, one pure fold:\n   158\t\n   159\t1. **Admit elements.** Each element is a d5 row or a\n   160\t   `{:dao.space/transaction {:t :datoms}}` record, flattened. Admission is\n   161\t   *looser than* `datom/local-datom?`, which `element-datoms` applies today\n   162\t   and which requires a non-negative `e`: in `:unresolved` mode a negative\n   163\t   `e`, and a negative `v` under an attribute `:schema` declares a ref, are\n   164\t   admitted as **tempids** (integer `t ≥ 0`, integer `m`, namespaced keyword\n   165\t   `a` are still required). In `:resolved` mode admission *is*\n   166\t   `local-datom?`. Strictness is restored after step 2: every row that\n   167\t   reaches step 3 satisfies `local-datom?`. Anything not admitted is a\n   168\t   **defect** — recorded in `:defects` with the batch ordinal and the\n   169\t   element's position — and the whole batch is skipped *without* stopping\n   170\t   the session. (A throwing `load` would leave the cursor on the bad batch\n   171\t   forever — the same head-of-line hazard `yin.vm.macro.md` decision 11\n   172\t   removes. Bad input is data; a bug in the fold itself is the throw.)\n   173\t   `datoms-from-elements` keeps its strict, throwing contract for the\n   174\t   one-shot path; the fold uses an admitting, non-throwing variant of the\n   175\t   same per-element rule.\n   176\t2. **Resolve identity** (§3). `:unresolved` mode: every tempid in the batch\n   177\t   is mapped through a batch-local table to a fresh durable id from `:ids`,\n   178\t   and the mapping is emitted as resolution facts. `:resolved` mode: nothing\n   179\t   is allocated; ids pass through. A batch that violates its mode (a tempid\n   180\t   on a `:resolved` medium; a positive `e` on an `:unresolved` one) is a\n   181\t   defect for the whole batch, so a medium can never be half-resolved.\n   182\t3. **Fold.** Rows and resolution facts are `dao.data.btree/conj`ed into the\n   183\t   four trees — persistent insert, structurally shared with the previous\n   184\t   state, and *dirty-tracked* against the trees' `:storage` so that §4.1's\n   185\t   `store-tree` later emits only what this and subsequent folds changed.\n   186\t   Covered indexes are sets; a duplicate row is a no-op.\n   187\t4. **Advance** `:batch`, and `:ids` in `:unresolved` mode.\n   188\t\n   189\tThe `:indexes` after batch *n* are a pure function of (index state before,\n   190\tbatch), on every host.\n   191\t\n   192\t### 2.3 What the two views mean over each medium\n   193\t\n   194\t`dao.space.query/current` and `history` are unchanged: they are\n   195\tinterpreters over rows, and the index state's trees are a row source.\n   196\t\n   197\t| Medium | `t` in rows | `current` | `history` |\n   198\t|---|---|---|---|\n   199\t| transactor-written (records) | allocated by the writer, monotonic | greatest-`t`-wins per `[e a v]`, retractions removed — full Datomic semantics | exact rows in `t` order |\n   200\t| a medium whose rows carry no writer-allocated `t` (all rows `t 0`, `m default-op`) | not meaningful | the *set* of facts asserted — correct for a medium of assertions with no retractions | degenerate: all rows share `t 0`; order is the observer's batch ordinal, available through resolution facts (§3), **not** through `t` |\n   201\t\n   202\tThe observer does not paper over the second row by minting `t` — decision\n   203\t4. A composition that wants transaction-time semantics over such a medium\n   204\troutes it through a transactor (the writer allocates `t`); one that wants\n   205\t\"what facts does this medium assert\" reads `current` and gets exactly that.\n   206\t\n   207\t---\n   208\t\n   209\t## 3. Identity\n   210\t\n   211\t### 3.1 Why the observer must resolve\n   212\t\n   213\tA medium written batch by batch without a transactor carries negative\n   214\ttempids allocated *per batch* (from `(- datom/first-user-id)` downward, by\n   215\tconvention; the observer relies only on their being negative). Two\n   216\tconsecutive batches both contain `-16`. Folding them raw into one index\n   217\twould merge unrelated entities — the silent-wrong-data case.\n   218\t`dao.space.transact` resolves tempids within one transaction; the observer\n   219\tmust do the same per batch, and only the observer can, because only it\n   220\tholds the cross-batch allocator.\n   221\t\n   222\t**Why a mode, fixed at construction.** Durable ids are positive integers on\n   223\tone number line. A medium whose writer allocates positive ids (a transactor's\n   224\tlog) and an observer minting positive ids for tempids cannot share an index\n   225\twithout eventually colliding: the writer emits 100, the observer mints 101,\n   226\tthe writer's next transaction emits 101. No watermark rule fixes this,\n   227\tbecause the two allocators never coordinate. So an index state is either\n   228\t`:resolved` — it indexes media whose ids are already durable and allocates\n   229\tnothing — or `:unresolved` — it indexes media that carry only tempids and\n   230\towns every positive id in its index, allocating from `datom/first-user-id`\n   231\tupward. A composition that needs both kinds of medium in one query opens two\n   232\tindex states as two sources; `dao.space.query` already keeps sources as\n   233\tseparate db-values and joins only where the query says so — and here the\n   234\tquery must *not* equate `?e` across a `:resolved` and an `:unresolved`\n   235\tsource, since both number lines start at `datom/first-user-id` and the same\n   236\tinteger names unrelated entities. Joins across them go through values, never\n   237\tids. Phase 2's shared allocator is therefore a shared allocator across\n   238\t`:unresolved` sessions only.\n   239\t\n   240\t### 3.2 Resolution facts\n   241\t\n   242\tFor every tempid `τ` the observer maps to durable `δ` in batch *n*, it\n   243\tasserts, in its own attribute namespace:\n   244\t\n   245\t```\n   246\t[δ :dao.space.index/batch   n   n default-op]\n   247\t[δ :dao.space.index/tempid  τ   n default-op]\n   248\t```\n   249\t\n   250\tThe row's `t` is the observer's batch ordinal *n* — its own logical clock,\n   251\tnever a host clock, and not the observed rows' `t`. `m` is\n   252\t`datom/default-op`: the attribute namespace alone distinguishes these facts\n   253\tfrom observed rows, and nothing else is needed. (An earlier draft gave the\n   254\tobserver \"its own operation entity\" for `m`; that would have required\n   255\tallocating an entity for the observer itself, and the namespace already\n   256\tdoes the work.) These are facts *about* observation, distinct from the\n   257\tobserved rows. They are what make the following queryable:\n   258\t\n   259\t- **Which batch asserted this entity** — `[?e :dao.space.index/batch ?n]`.\n   260\t- **Cross-medium provenance**, the case `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2 leaves to\n   261\t  \"a composition that commits\". With one index session per medium\n   262\t  (`program-in`, `program-out`, log) sharing an allocator, or one session\n   263\t  over a merged view, the macro expander's event\n   264\t  `[ev :yin/source-batch t] [ev :yin/source-call -16]` joins to the source\n   265\t  entity through `[?e :dao.space.index/batch t'] [?e\n   266\t  :dao.space.index/tempid -16]` — where `t'` is the observer's ordinal\n   267\t  for the source batch the expander numbered `t`. The correspondence\n   268\t  between the expander's `:t` and the observer's ordinal is one more\n   269\t  resolution fact the composition asserts when it wires both observers to\n   270\t  the same medium. It is a constant offset **only while neither session has\n   271\t  seen a `gap`**: a skipped batch on either side shifts every later ordinal\n   272\t  by one, silently. The composition must therefore check both sessions'\n   273\t  `:ingress-gaps` before trusting the offset, and after a gap either\n   274\t  re-derive it or mark provenance across that point as unknown. This is the\n   275\t  same \"an index with a gap is a partial index\" rule as open question 2.\n   276\t\n   277\tRefs are resolved only for attributes the supplied `:schema` declares as\n   278\t`:db.type/ref` — exactly as the transactor relocates only declared refs\n   279\ttoday. The schema is the composition's to supply (for a program medium,\n   280\t`yin.vm/schema`; for an expander's log, `yin.vm.macro/event-schema`);\n   281\tthe index learns which attributes are refs from it and nothing else. An undeclared attribute holding a\n   282\tnegative number is a value, not a ref, and is left alone.\n   283\t\n   284\t### 3.3 What is not promised\n   285\t\n   286\tDurable ids are the *observer's* reading. Two independent observers over the\n   287\tsame medium allocate independently; their ids agree only if they are the\n   288\tsame session or share a checkpoint (§4.2). This is the same statement as\n   289\t`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2's \"refs across media are meaningful only for durable\n   290\tids\", now with the owner of \"durable\" named: it is whichever dao.space\n   291\tobserver indexed the medium.\n   292\t\n   293\t---\n   294\t\n   295\t## 4. Publication and the checkpoint\n   296\t\n   297\t### 4.1 Incremental publication\n   298\t\n   299\tBecause `:indexes` are persistent `dao.data.btree` values, publishing after\n   300\tbatch *n* is `bt/store-tree` over each tree plus one manifest — the same\n   301\tnode-blob format and intake path `publish-index!` uses today. What makes it\n   302\t*incremental* is that the trees keep their `:storage` across publishes:\n   303\t`store-tree` stores only the **dirty subgraph** (its docstring: \"no-op\n   304\treturning the existing address when the set is already stored\"), so nodes\n   305\tunchanged since the last publication are never re-stored and never\n   306\tre-appended. `publish-index!` today rebuilds into a *fresh* recording\n   307\thandle each time, which is why every node is dirty and every publish is\n   308\tO(tree). Two things persist across publishes in the index state, and they\n   309\tmust not be confused: the **stored-address marks on the trees' nodes**,\n   310\twhich are what `store-tree` consults (dirtiness lives in the tree, not in\n   311\tthe storage), and the **identity of `:storage`**, so that a stored node is\n   312\tnever re-stored under a second handle. What does *not* persist is the\n   313\trecording handle's content: the blobs it recorded since the last flush are\n   314\texactly the payloads to append, in store order, and `flush-staged` drains\n   315\tthem once the intake has accepted the manifest, so the handle never grows\n   316\twith history. Append cost is therefore proportional to what changed;\n   317\t`jing/materialize!`'s dedup at the far end is a second line of defence, not\n   318\tthe mechanism.\n   319\t\n   320\tThe staging discipline is `yin.vm.macro.md` §5's: node blobs then manifest\n   321\tare staged as one payload list; `full` retains the exact list and leaves the\n   322\tindex state not-ready; `ok` on the manifest clears it. The manifest is always\n   323\tlast, so a partial prefix is retry-safe, as today.\n   324\t\n   325\t### 4.2 The checkpoint the transactor asked for\n   326\t\n   327\t`dao.space.transactor.md` *Open items*: \"the log carries its own checkpoint,\n   328\tfrom which the watermark and incremental indexes resume\". An index state\n   329\tis that checkpoint's shape: `{:manifest-address a :cursor c :ids i\n   330\t:batch n}` — the published trees, the medium position they cover, and the\n   331\tallocator state. A restarted observer opens the manifest (lazy, via\n   332\t`query/open-published!`), re-attaches at `c`, and continues folding. Nothing\n   333\tis replayed from the origin. The watermark the transactor derives by\n   334\tscanning is `(max t)` over the checkpointed index's rows — a query, not a\n   335\tscan.\n   336\t\n   337\tTwo caveats bound this. First, a cursor is transport-scoped: it names a\n   338\tposition on *this* handle's logical stream. Over a process-lifetime\n   339\tmemory-log — the transactor's local medium today — a restarted process sees\n   340\t\"a new, empty logical stream with a new identity\" (`dao.space.md`, *Fault\n   341\tTolerance*), so the checkpoint's `:cursor` is meaningless after restart and\n   342\ta resumed observer over such a medium can only start a fresh session at\n   343\t`:oldest` of the new, empty log. Resumption in the sense above needs a\n   344\tmedium whose cursors survive the process (`dao.stream.file.md`), and the\n   345\tcheckpoint is worth recording only for those. Second, whether the checkpoint\n   346\trecord lives on the observed medium itself (the transactor doc's preference:\n   347\t\"one truth\") or beside the manifest in `dao.jing` is left open here; either\n   348\tis a composition choice, and the observer's state is the same value in\n   349\tboth.\n   350\t\n   351\t---\n   352\t\n   353\t## 5. Relationship to the transactor and to `publish-index!`\n   354\t\n   355\tThe transactor remains the writer and the owner of `t`. \"Indexing is the\n   356\twriter's duty\" becomes: *the writer runs `dao.space.index` as an observer\n   357\tover its own stream* — one index state attached to its local memory-log,\n   358\tpublishing on its policy. `publish-index!` is then a convenience over the\n   359\tsame functions: attach at `:oldest`, `fold-batch` to `blocked`, publish\n   360\tonce, discard the state. Its full-history behaviour is preserved as the\n   361\tdegenerate case of an observer that never kept state, and its manifest\n   362\tformat, intake path, and retry-safety are unchanged.\n   363\t\n   364\t`dao.space.index` gains `fold-batch`, `publish!`, `flush-staged`, `drain`,\n   365\tthe admitting per-element rule, and the resolution-fact attributes; it loses\n   366\tnothing. Nothing about `dao.jing`\n   367\tchanges: it still materializes opaque payloads from intake pools and never\n   368\tinterprets. Nothing about `dao.space.query` changes: the index state's trees\n   369\tare one more row source, alongside relation values and published\n   370\tmanifests. The three boundaries of `dao.space.md` are untouched;\n   371\twhat moves is *when* the transactor-side index is built (continuously,\n   372\tnot at publish) and *over what* (any medium, not only the writer's own).\n   373\t\n   374\t---\n   375\t\n   376\t## 6. Invariants\n   377\t\n   378\t| Invariant | How the observer honours it |\n   379\t|---|---|\n   380\t| No hidden global state | Allocator, batch ordinal, trees, staged publication — all fields of one index-state value threaded through `run-on-stream`. No registry of observers; a composition holds the states it created. |\n   381\t| No implicit control flow | Indexing happens in exactly one place: `fold-batch` inside the observe step. Nothing indexes on write, on query, or on publish. |\n   382\t| No callbacks | `fold-batch` is a function (index-state, batch) → index-state'. The driver is `run-on-stream`; cadence is the composition's. |\n   383\t| No shared mutable state | `dao.data.btree` values are persistent; two sessions never share a tree by reference they could both mutate. The recording content handle at publish time is created per publication. |\n   384\t| No layer collapsing | *append* (writer) → medium → *observe/fold* (this note) → *publish* (intake) → *materialize* (`dao.jing`) → *query*. The VM is a sibling observer, not a stage. |\n   385\t| No assumed graphs | Ref resolution uses only the supplied `:schema`; the observer does not infer which values are refs. Batch shape is validated by `datoms-from-elements`; malformed elements are defects, not guesses. |\n   386\t\n   387\t---\n   388\t\n   389\t## 7. Phases\n   390\t\n   391\t**Phase 0 — relocate the coordination loop (done 2026-09-13).** The\n   392\t`attach`/`observe-next`/`run-on-stream` loop lived at\n   393\t`yin.vm.stream-observer` because the VM was its first consumer, but its\n   394\town docstring says it inspects no evaluator field, and a `dao.space`\n   395\tnamespace requiring `yin.vm.*` would invert the layering (`dao.stream` →\n   396\t`dao.space` → `yin.vm`, never back). It is now `dao.stream.observer`\n   397\t(`src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc`, test\n   398\t`test/dao/stream/observer_test.cljc`), beside `dao.stream.observe`\n   399\twhose single `step` it loops over. The `yin.vm` evaluators, the REPL, and\n   400\tthe macro expander require it from there; `yin.vm` keeps no observer code\n   401\tof its own. The pending fix from `yin.vm.macro.md` §5 — keep the throw,\n   402\tcarry the partial `{:observer :consumer}` session in `ex-data` — lands in this\n   403\tnamespace.\n   404\t\n   405\t**Phase 0′ — parity over the transactor's medium.** `dao.space.index` gains\n   406\t`fold-batch`, `publish!`, `flush-staged`, `drain`, the admitting element\n   407\trule, and the resolution facts; a `:resolved` session attached at `:oldest`\n   408\tover a transactor's local memory-log produces `:indexes` equal (as sets) to\n   409\t`index-datoms` over `snapshot-datoms` of the same log, and a one-shot\n   410\t`publish!` equal (as a manifest) to `publish-index!`. Tests: set equality on\n   411\tall four trees; manifest equality; a malformed element becomes a defect and\n   412\tthe next batch still folds; a tempid on a `:resolved` medium is a defect;\n   413\t`run-on-stream` `blocked`/`end` behaviour; a second `publish!` after more\n   414\tbatches appends only the blobs stored since the first (count them); ids\n   415\tstable across a `pr-str`/`read-string` round trip of the session (it is\n   416\tplain data plus btree values).\n   417\t\n   418\t**Phase 1 — a medium with batch-local tempids (composition test).** The\n   419\tindex is exercised over a medium another observer also reads: two\n   420\t`dao.stream.observer` sessions on one `program-out`, one driving\n   421\t`ast-walker`, one driving `dao.space.index` in `:unresolved` mode. The\n   422\tindex code under test knows nothing of the VM; the test does. Tests:\n   423\tnegative `e` rows are admitted and resolved, positive `e` on this medium is\n   424\ta defect; the VM's value and the\n   425\tindex's `current` view from the same batches; after each appended batch,\n   426\twith no snapshot call, `q` over the index state already sees that batch's\n   427\tentities; two batches with overlapping\n   428\ttempids index as distinct entities with correct resolution facts;\n   429\t`[?e :yin/type :lambda]` finds every lambda the program contained; a ref\n   430\tattribute resolved, an undeclared negative value untouched; `history`\n   431\tdocumented as degenerate over `t 0` rows.\n   432\t\n   433\t**Phase 2 — cross-medium provenance.** Observers over `program-in`,\n   434\t`program-out`, and the macro log with a shared allocator; the offset fact\n   435\tbetween the expander's `:t` and the observer's ordinal. Test: the\n   436\t`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 query chain source-call → event → expansion-root\n   437\tanswered by `dao.space.query/q` over the three indexed media.\n   438\t\n   439\t**Phase 3 — checkpoint over a durable medium.** Over a file-backed medium\n   440\t(`dao.stream.file`), restart from `{:manifest-address :cursor :ids :batch}`\n   441\tand continue; the resumed index equals a from-origin index. Over a\n   442\tmemory-log, assert the documented behaviour instead: a resumed session\n   443\tstarts fresh at `:oldest` of the new log and the checkpoint is not\n   444\tconsulted. The transactor's `create!` derives its watermark from a\n   445\tcheckpoint when one is offered.\n   446\t\n   447\t---\n   448\t\n   449\t## 8. Open questions\n   450\t\n   451\t- **One session per medium, or one over a merged view?** Per medium keeps\n   452\t  each cursor honest and makes `gap` attributable; a merged view needs a\n   453\t  merge interpreter that `dao.stream.md` does not (and should not) provide.\n   454\t  This note leans per-medium with a shared allocator, but the allocator\n   455\t  then couples the sessions — a composition choice to state when it is\n   456\t  made.\n   457\t- **Retention and `gap`.** Over an evicting transport the observer inherits\n   458\t  `dao.stream.observer`'s gap accounting; an index with a gap is a partial\n   459\t  index and must say so, and a cross-session ordinal offset (§3.2) is void\n   460\t  after one. Whether a published manifest should carry the gap count is\n   461\t  undecided.\n   462\t- **Where the checkpoint lives** (§4.2).\n   463\t- **Whether `snapshot` is retired** in favour of \"run a throwaway session to\n   464\t  `blocked` and take its trees\". Probably, but `snapshot`'s status\n   465\t  vocabulary (`:ended`/`:blocked`/`:gap`/`:defect`) should survive as the\n   466\t  session's terminal report.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
