{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc'","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.space.index\n     2\t  \"The transactor-side indexing library (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Publication\n     3\t   from an agent).\n     4\t\n     5\t   In dao.space every agent appending to its own `dao.stream` is its own\n     6\t   transactor, so every agent also owns indexing its own datoms. This\n     7\t   library is that duty, the write-side peer of `dao.space.query` (the\n     8\t   embeddable reader that consumes what this publishes):\n     9\t\n    10\t     - `publish-index!` snapshots the agent's local stream, builds the four\n    11\t       covered indexes as immutable content-addressed `dao.data.btree` node\n    12\t       blobs, and appends them (children before parents, manifest last) to\n    13\t       one intake stream selected from an explicit pool. A DaoJing observer\n    14\t       over the pool materializes the blobs into content-addressed storage.\n    15\t     - `read-manifest` / `read-datoms` read a published manifest back and\n    16\t       walk its EAVT node graph eagerly; `restored-indexes` re-attaches the\n    17\t       manifest's trees lazily.\n    18\t\n    19\t   It owns the index *realization* both sides share:\n    20\t\n    21\t     - the sort orders (`eavt-cmp`/`aevt-cmp`/`avet-cmp`/`vaet-cmp` over\n    22\t       heterogeneous values) and the in-memory index (`index-datoms`,\n    23\t       `subseq-from`)\n    24\t     - the persisted node-blob format, both directions: nodes store as\n    25\t       plain-EDN content-addressed segment blobs (Merkle by construction —\n    26\t       dao.data.btree stores children before parents); the manifest is\n    27\t       exactly `{:indexes {:eavt addr-or-nil :aevt ... :avet ... :vaet ...}\n    28\t       :count n :branching-factor n}` — no source stream, no pool, no epoch,\n    29\t       no own address.\n    30\t\n    31\t   Build and lazy restore run on every platform: the tree is dao.data.btree,\n    32\t   one .cljc source (JVM, cljs, cljd), and durability is its IStorage over a\n    33\t   content-store handle (dao.data.btree.storage). Node blobs are ordinary\n    34\t   EDN either way.\"\n    35\t  (:require [dao.data.btree :as bt]\n    36\t            [dao.data.btree.storage :as bts]\n    37\t            [dao.datom :as datom]\n    38\t            [dao.jing :as jing]\n    39\t            [dao.stream :as stream]))\n    40\t\n    41\t\n    42\t;; =============================================================================\n    43\t;; Value comparison (heterogeneous datom values)\n    44\t;; =============================================================================\n    45\t\n    46\t(defn- type-rank\n    47\t  [x]\n    48\t  (cond (nil? x) 0\n    49\t        (boolean? x) 1\n    50\t        (number? x) 2\n    51\t        (string? x) 3\n    52\t        (keyword? x) 4\n    53\t        (symbol? x) 5\n    54\t        :else 6))\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\t(defn compare-vals\n    58\t  \"Compare two datom values across heterogeneous types using type-rank.\"\n    59\t  [a b]\n    60\t  (let [ra (type-rank a)\n    61\t        rb (type-rank b)]\n    62\t    (if (= ra rb)\n    63\t      (try (compare a b)\n    64\t           ;; :cljd FIRST in every reader-conditional: the cljd host-eval\n    65\t           ;; pass also matches :clj, so a :clj branch appearing earlier\n    66\t           ;; in the clause list wins on cljd too — here that would mean\n    67\t           ;; referencing the JVM-only ClassCastException, which doesn't\n    68\t           ;; exist in Dart.\n    69\t           (catch #?(:cljd Object\n    70\t                     :clj ClassCastException\n    71\t                     :cljs js/Error)\n    72\t                  _\n    73\t             (compare (str a) (str b))))\n    74\t      (compare ra rb))))\n    75\t\n    76\t\n    77\t;; =============================================================================\n    78\t;; Datom slots and sort orders\n    79\t;; =============================================================================\n    80\t\n    81\t(defn datom-e\n    82\t  [d]\n    83\t  (nth d 0))\n    84\t\n    85\t\n    86\t(defn datom-a\n    87\t  [d]\n    88\t  (nth d 1))\n    89\t\n    90\t\n    91\t(defn datom-v\n    92\t  [d]\n    93\t  (nth d 2))\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\t(defn datom-t\n    97\t  [d]\n    98\t  (nth d 3))\n    99\t\n   100\t\n   101\t(defn datom-m\n   102\t  [d]\n   103\t  (nth d 4))\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn- cmp-field\n   107\t  \"Nil-first, heterogeneous-safe field comparison. Entity ids are not\n   108\t   guaranteed to be integers in query-only db-values, where a raw entity\n   109\t   map's :db/id is caller-chosen, so every slot, not just v, needs\n   110\t   compare-vals. Persisted local datoms are validated separately.\"\n   111\t  [a b]\n   112\t  (cond (nil? a) (if (nil? b) 0 -1)\n   113\t        (nil? b) 1\n   114\t        :else (compare-vals a b)))\n   115\t\n   116\t\n   117\t;; Covered indexes order canonical local d5 datoms only. Stream scope belongs\n   118\t;; to the interpreter selecting a source, not to an appended tuple slot.\n   119\t\n   120\t(defn eavt-cmp\n   121\t  [d1 d2]\n   122\t  (let [c (cmp-field (datom-e d1) (datom-e d2))]\n   123\t    (if (zero? c)\n   124\t      (let [c (cmp-field (datom-a d1) (datom-a d2))]\n   125\t        (if (zero? c)\n   126\t          (let [c (cmp-field (datom-v d1) (datom-v d2))]\n   127\t            (if (zero? c)\n   128\t              (let [c (cmp-field (datom-t d1) (datom-t d2))]\n   129\t                (if (zero? c) (cmp-field (datom-m d1) (datom-m d2)) c))\n   130\t              c))\n   131\t          c))\n   132\t      c)))\n   133\t\n   134\t\n   135\t(defn aevt-cmp\n   136\t  [d1 d2]\n   137\t  (let [c (cmp-field (datom-a d1) (datom-a d2))]\n   138\t    (if (zero? c)\n   139\t      (let [c (cmp-field (datom-e d1) (datom-e d2))]\n   140\t        (if (zero? c)\n   141\t          (let [c (cmp-field (datom-v d1) (datom-v d2))]\n   142\t            (if (zero? c)\n   143\t              (let [c (cmp-field (datom-t d1) (datom-t d2))]\n   144\t                (if (zero? c) (cmp-field (datom-m d1) (datom-m d2)) c))\n   145\t              c))\n   146\t          c))\n   147\t      c)))\n   148\t\n   149\t\n   150\t(defn avet-cmp\n   151\t  [d1 d2]\n   152\t  (let [c (cmp-field (datom-a d1) (datom-a d2))]\n   153\t    (if (zero? c)\n   154\t      (let [c (cmp-field (datom-v d1) (datom-v d2))]\n   155\t        (if (zero? c)\n   156\t          (let [c (cmp-field (datom-e d1) (datom-e d2))]\n   157\t            (if (zero? c)\n   158\t              (let [c (cmp-field (datom-t d1) (datom-t d2))]\n   159\t                (if (zero? c) (cmp-field (datom-m d1) (datom-m d2)) c))\n   160\t              c))\n   161\t          c))\n   162\t      c)))\n   163\t\n   164\t\n   165\t(defn vaet-cmp\n   166\t  \"VAET sort: v, a, e, t, m. Reverse-reference lookup — 'which datoms\n   167\t   point to this value.' Heterogeneous-safe (the ref value is caller-chosen\n   168\t   and can be any type, the same way entity ids are).\"\n   169\t  [d1 d2]\n   170\t  (let [c (cmp-field (datom-v d1) (datom-v d2))]\n   171\t    (if (zero? c)\n   172\t      (let [c (cmp-field (datom-a d1) (datom-a d2))]\n   173\t        (if (zero? c)\n   174\t          (let [c (cmp-field (datom-e d1) (datom-e d2))]\n   175\t            (if (zero? c)\n   176\t              (let [c (cmp-field (datom-t d1) (datom-t d2))]\n   177\t                (if (zero? c) (cmp-field (datom-m d1) (datom-m d2)) c))\n   178\t              c))\n   179\t          c))\n   180\t      c)))\n   181\t\n   182\t\n   183\t;; =============================================================================\n   184\t;; In-memory index\n   185\t;; =============================================================================\n   186\t\n   187\t(defn- sorted-index-by\n   188\t  [cmp]\n   189\t  (bt/sorted-set-by cmp))\n   190\t\n   191\t\n   192\t(defn subseq-from\n   193\t  \"All elements >= sentinel, in index order: a log-n slice descent that,\n   194\t   on a lazily-restored set, loads only the nodes on the seek path plus\n   195\t   the matching range, never the nodes left of the sentinel. One\n   196\t   implementation on every platform (dao.data.btree).\"\n   197\t  [sorted-set cmp sentinel]\n   198\t  (bt/slice sorted-set sentinel nil cmp))\n   199\t\n   200\t\n   201\t(defn index-datoms\n   202\t  \"Build {:eavt ... :aevt ... :avet ... :vaet ...} sorted indexes from a\n   203\t   seq of datoms.\"\n   204\t  [datoms]\n   205\t  {:eavt (into (sorted-index-by eavt-cmp) datoms),\n   206\t   :aevt (into (sorted-index-by aevt-cmp) datoms),\n   207\t   :avet (into (sorted-index-by avet-cmp) datoms),\n   208\t   :vaet (into (sorted-index-by vaet-cmp) datoms)})\n   209\t\n   210\t\n   211\t;; =============================================================================\n   212\t;; Persisted indexes — content-addressed B-Tree node blobs\n   213\t;; =============================================================================\n   214\t;; publish-index! stores the four covered indexes as immutable,\n   215\t;; content-addressed B-Tree node blobs (dao.data.btree stores children\n   216\t;; before parents, so the blob emission order is Merkle by construction)\n   217\t;; and appends them plus a manifest to one intake stream. A DaoJing observer\n   218\t;; materializes the blobs into a content store; consumers either walk the\n   219\t;; node graph eagerly with plain `jing/get` (walk-index-datoms /\n   220\t;; read-datoms) or re-attach the trees lazily through dao.data.btree.storage\n   221\t;; (restored-indexes). Both paths run on every platform.\n   222\t\n   223\t(def ^:private content-missing\n   224\t  \"Not-found sentinel for content-store reads. An opaque per-host identity\n   225\t   object, never a keyword: a keyword would be ambiguous with a genuinely\n   226\t   stored value.\"\n   227\t  #?(:cljd (Object.)\n   228\t     :clj (Object.)\n   229\t     :cljs (js-obj)))\n   230\t\n   231\t\n   232\t(defn walk-index-datoms\n   233\t  \"Eagerly collect every datom reachable from a persisted index node, in\n   234\t   index order, by walking the node graph with plain `jing/get`. Node blobs\n   235\t   are ordinary EDN maps (leaf `{:keys [...]}`, branch `{:level n :keys\n   236\t   [...] :addresses [...]}`), so this works on every platform — it needs no\n   237\t   tree-library support at all, only `jing/get` on plain EDN maps. A nil\n   238\t   address (an empty index) walks to ().\"\n   239\t  [store address]\n   240\t  (if (nil? address)\n   241\t    ()\n   242\t    (let [node (jing/get store address nil)]\n   243\t      (when (nil? node)\n   244\t        (throw (ex-info \"missing index segment\" {:address address})))\n   245\t      (if-some [addresses (:addresses node)]\n   246\t        (mapcat #(walk-index-datoms store %) addresses)\n   247\t        (:keys node)))))\n   248\t\n   249\t\n   250\t(defn- valid-manifest?\n   251\t  \"A conforming manifest is exactly {:indexes {:eavt ... :aevt ... :avet\n   252\t   ... :vaet ...} :count n :branching-factor n}, each index address either\n   253\t   nil when count is zero or a :segment/sha256-... content address when\n   254\t   count is positive. The B-tree branching factor is at least two.\"\n   255\t  [manifest]\n   256\t  (and (map? manifest)\n   257\t       (= #{:indexes :count :branching-factor} (set (keys manifest)))\n   258\t       (map? (:indexes manifest))\n   259\t       (= #{:eavt :aevt :avet :vaet} (set (keys (:indexes manifest))))\n   260\t       (integer? (:count manifest))\n   261\t       (not (neg? (:count manifest)))\n   262\t       (integer? (:branching-factor manifest))\n   263\t       (<= 2 (:branching-factor manifest))\n   264\t       (if (zero? (:count manifest))\n   265\t         (every? nil? (vals (:indexes manifest)))\n   266\t         (every? jing/segment-address? (vals (:indexes manifest))))))\n   267\t\n   268\t\n   269\t(defn read-manifest\n   270\t  \"Retrieve the manifest stored at a content address. Throws on a missing\n   271\t   address and on a stored value that is not a conforming manifest.\"\n   272\t  [content-store manifest-address]\n   273\t  (let [manifest (jing/get content-store manifest-address content-missing)]\n   274\t    (when (identical? manifest content-missing)\n   275\t      (throw (ex-info \"missing index manifest\" {:address manifest-address})))\n   276\t    (when-not (valid-manifest? manifest)\n   277\t      (throw (ex-info \"invalid index manifest\"\n   278\t                      {:address manifest-address, :manifest manifest})))\n   279\t    (let [actual-address (jing/segment-key manifest)]\n   280\t      (when-not (= manifest-address actual-address)\n   281\t        (throw (ex-info \"index manifest content address mismatch\"\n   282\t                        {:expected manifest-address,\n   283\t                         :actual actual-address,\n   284\t                         :manifest manifest}))))\n   285\t    manifest))\n   286\t\n   287\t\n   288\t(defn read-datoms\n   289\t  \"Eagerly read every datom in the EAVT index of a published manifest, in\n   290\t   index order, by walking the node graph with plain `jing/get`. Takes the\n   291\t   manifest's content address; an empty index reads as ().\"\n   292\t  [content-store manifest-address]\n   293\t  (let [manifest (read-manifest content-store manifest-address)]\n   294\t    (vec (walk-index-datoms content-store (:eavt (:indexes manifest))))))\n   295\t\n   296\t\n   297\t(defn restored-indexes\n   298\t  \"Lazily-loaded {:eavt :aevt :avet :vaet} dao.data.btree sets over a\n   299\t   published manifest (`{:indexes {...} :count n :branching-factor n}`).\n   300\t   Nothing is fetched until a query traverses; slice (subseq-from) then\n   301\t   loads only the seek path plus the matching range. Works on every\n   302\t   platform.\n   303\t\n   304\t   The manifest's :count and :branching-factor are threaded through\n   305\t   restore-tree deliberately: count keeps O(1) `count` on restored trees\n   306\t   without faulting the graph, and the branching factor reaches every\n   307\t   restored node so mutation splits at the published thresholds. A manifest\n   308\t   without :count or :branching-factor is foreign or hand-built and belongs\n   309\t   to the eager path (walk-index-datoms), not here.\"\n   310\t  [content-store manifest]\n   311\t  (let [{:keys [indexes count branching-factor]} manifest\n   312\t        storage (bts/kv-storage content-store\n   313\t                                {:branching-factor (or branching-factor 512)})]\n   314\t    {:eavt (bt/restore-tree eavt-cmp (:eavt indexes) storage count),\n   315\t     :aevt (bt/restore-tree aevt-cmp (:aevt indexes) storage count),\n   316\t     :avet (bt/restore-tree avet-cmp (:avet indexes) storage count),\n   317\t     :vaet (bt/restore-tree vaet-cmp (:vaet indexes) storage count)}))\n   318\t\n   319\t\n   320\t;; =============================================================================\n   321\t;; The published read coordinate\n   322\t;; =============================================================================\n   323\t\n   324\t(defn published-index\n   325\t  \"Construct a transportable descriptor for one immutable covered-index\n   326\t   manifest. `content-store` is a serializable DaoJing coordinate, not a live\n   327\t   handle. The manifest address is the exact bound; opening the coordinate is\n   328\t   the reader's move — dao.space.query's open-published! re-attaches the\n   329\t   covered sets lazily and defers the EAVT rows, read-datoms below walks them\n   330\t   eagerly.\"\n   331\t  [content-store manifest-address]\n   332\t  (when-not (and (map? content-store) (keyword? (:dao.jing/type content-store)))\n   333\t    (throw (ex-info \"published index requires a DaoJing store coordinate\"\n   334\t                    {:content-store content-store})))\n   335\t  (when-not (jing/segment-address? manifest-address)\n   336\t    (throw (ex-info \"published index requires a manifest content address\"\n   337\t                    {:manifest-address manifest-address})))\n   338\t  {:dao.stream/type :dao.space.index/published,\n   339\t   :dao.stream/bound {:manifest-address manifest-address},\n   340\t   :dao.stream/comparator :dao.space.index/eavt,\n   341\t   :content-store content-store,\n   342\t   :manifest-address manifest-address})\n   343\t\n   344\t\n   345\t(defn covered-indexes\n   346\t  \"Return the {:eavt :aevt :avet :vaet} covered d5 sets carried in a\n   347\t   realization's :indexes field, or nil when the realization carries none.\n   348\t   Structural check only: the realization must be a map whose :indexes field\n   349\t   is a map with exactly those four keys, so it is clj/cljs/cljd-portable and\n   350\t   never an instance/type check. It means the realization carries covered d5\n   351\t   sets, not that it is lazy: an in-memory index-datoms map is structurally\n   352\t   indistinguishable, and that is fine (resolution happens the same way\n   353\t   either way).\"\n   354\t  [realization]\n   355\t  (when (map? realization)\n   356\t    (let [indexes (:indexes realization)]\n   357\t      (when (and (map? indexes)\n   358\t                 (= #{:eavt :aevt :avet :vaet} (set (keys indexes))))\n   359\t        indexes))))\n   360\t\n   361\t\n   362\t;; =============================================================================\n   363\t;; publish-index! — build, record, append\n   364\t;; =============================================================================\n   365\t\n   366\t(defn- element-datoms\n   367\t  [payload]\n   368\t  (if (and (map? payload) (contains? payload :dao.space/transaction))\n   369\t    (let [tx (:dao.space/transaction payload)\n   370\t          t (:t tx)\n   371\t          datoms (:datoms tx)]\n   372\t      (when-not (and (map? tx)\n   373\t                     (= #{:t :datoms} (set (keys tx)))\n   374\t                     (integer? t)\n   375\t                     (not (neg? t))\n   376\t                     (vector? datoms)\n   377\t                     (seq datoms)\n   378\t                     (every? datom/local-datom? datoms)\n   379\t                     (every? #(= t (datom-t %)) datoms))\n   380\t        (throw (ex-info \"malformed dao.space transaction record\"\n   381\t                        {:payload payload})))\n   382\t      datoms)\n   383\t    (cond\n   384\t      (datom/local-datom? payload) [payload]\n   385\t      (and (vector? payload) (= 5 (count payload)))\n   386\t      (throw\n   387\t        (ex-info\n   388\t          \"malformed local datom: e and t must be non-negative integers, m an integer, and a a namespaced keyword\"\n   389\t          {:datom payload}))\n   390\t      :else\n   391\t      (throw\n   392\t        (ex-info\n   393\t          \"local stream payload must be a datom or dao.space transaction record\"\n   394\t          {:payload payload})))))\n   395\t\n   396\t\n   397\t(defn datoms-from-elements\n   398\t  \"Flatten a seq of local-stream elements to canonical local d5 datoms —\n   399\t   the payload vocabulary's public, seq-level spelling. Each element is\n   400\t   validated and flattened by the same per-element rule snapshot-datoms\n   401\t   applies inside its read loop: a canonical datom vector passes through, an\n   402\t   atomic {:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}} record flattens to\n   403\t   its datoms, and anything malformed throws.\"\n   404\t  [elements]\n   405\t  (into [] (mapcat element-datoms) elements))\n   406\t\n   407\t\n   408\t(defn- checked\n   409\t  \"Fold a defective result into an exception before anything is read from it.\n   410\t   The contract's own validator (dao.stream/validate-outcome) answers nil\n   411\t   for a conforming result and a defect map otherwise; interpreting an\n   412\t   unvalidated result is how a loop ends up recurring on a nil cursor.\"\n   413\t  [operation result]\n   414\t  (if-let [defect (stream/validate-outcome operation result)]\n   415\t    (throw (ex-info \"malformed local stream result\"\n   416\t                    {:operation operation, :result result, :defect defect}))\n   417\t    result))\n   418\t\n   419\t\n   420\t(defn snapshot-datoms\n   421\t  \"Read an agent-local stream in full and flatten it to canonical local d5\n   422\t   datoms.\n   423\t\n   424\t   The local stream is on a complete-retention transport\n   425\t   (dao.stream.memory-log), so a fresh :oldest cursor is the origin and\n   426\t   `gap` cannot occur — completeness comes from the transport's declared\n   427\t   retention, never from the anchor (dao.stream.md, *Complete history*).\n   428\t   `blocked` (an open stream caught up) and `end` (a closed one fully read)\n   429\t   finish the read at the tail. Each element is validated and flattened as it\n   430\t   is read, so the first defect in stream order is the one reported and no\n   431\t   read happens past it. A well-formed but unexpected outcome means the handle\n   432\t   is not the transport the composition owes.\"\n   433\t  [local-stream]\n   434\t  (let [mint (checked :cursor (stream/cursor local-stream :dao.stream/oldest))]\n   435\t    (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome mint))\n   436\t      (throw (ex-info \"local stream refused an :oldest cursor\" {:result mint})))\n   437\t    (loop [cursor (:dao.stream/cursor mint)\n   438\t           datoms []]\n   439\t      (let [r (checked :next (stream/next local-stream cursor))]\n   440\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   441\t          :dao.stream/ok\n   442\t          (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r)\n   443\t                 (into datoms (element-datoms (:dao.stream/value r))))\n   444\t          (:dao.stream/blocked :dao.stream/end) datoms\n   445\t          (throw (ex-info \"local stream is not a complete-retention transport\"\n   446\t                          {:outcome (:dao.stream/outcome r), :result r})))))))\n   447\t\n   448\t\n   449\t(defn- recording-content-handle\n   450\t  \"Temporary in-memory content store for the publish build. :put-content-fn\n   451\t   records each unique node blob on first insertion (answering :present for\n   452\t   duplicates, so the recorded order is first-insertion order, deduplicated);\n   453\t   :get-content-fn reads recorded blobs back. Addresses are minted by\n   454\t   jing/materialize! through dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage, so the\n   455\t   recorded order is exactly the store-tree children-before-parent traversal.\n   456\t   The handle is a build-time value: no global state is introduced.\"\n   457\t  []\n   458\t  (let [state (atom {:content {}, :order []})]\n   459\t    {:state state,\n   460\t     :put-content-fn (fn [address payload]\n   461\t                       (if (contains? (:content @state) address)\n   462\t                         :present\n   463\t                         (do (swap! state\n   464\t                                    (fn [s]\n   465\t                                      (-> s\n   466\t                                          (assoc-in [:content address] payload)\n   467\t                                          (update :order conj [address payload]))))\n   468\t                             :inserted))),\n   469\t     :get-content-fn (fn [address not-found]\n   470\t                       (get-in @state [:content address] not-found))}))\n   471\t\n   472\t\n   473\t(defn- validate-branching!\n   474\t  [branching]\n   475\t  (when-not (and (integer? branching) (<= 2 branching))\n   476\t    (throw (ex-info\n   477\t             \"publish-index! :branching-factor must be an integer of at least 2\"\n   478\t             {:branching-factor branching})))\n   479\t  branching)\n   480\t\n   481\t\n   482\t(defn- select-intake-stream!\n   483\t  [intake-pool select-fn]\n   484\t  (when-not (coll? intake-pool)\n   485\t    (throw\n   486\t      (ex-info\n   487\t        \"publish-index! intake-pool must be a collection of writable dao.stream values\"\n   488\t        {:intake-pool intake-pool})))\n   489\t  (when (empty? intake-pool)\n   490\t    (throw (ex-info \"publish-index! intake-pool must be non-empty\"\n   491\t                    {:intake-pool intake-pool})))\n   492\t  (when-not (fn? select-fn)\n   493\t    (throw (ex-info\n   494\t             \"publish-index! :select-stream must be a function of the pool\"\n   495\t             {:select-stream select-fn})))\n   496\t  (let [selected (select-fn intake-pool)]\n   497\t    (when-not (boolean (some #(identical? % selected) intake-pool))\n   498\t      (throw\n   499\t        (ex-info\n   500\t          \"publish-index! :select-stream returned a value outside the intake pool\"\n   501\t          {:selected selected, :intake-pool intake-pool})))\n   502\t    selected))\n   503\t\n   504\t\n   505\t(defn- append-ok!\n   506\t  \"Append one opaque payload to an intake stream; every stream.v2/append!\n   507\t   must answer `:dao.stream/ok`, anything else throws with the result\n   508\t   attached.\"\n   509\t  [stream payload]\n   510\t  (let [result (stream/append! stream payload)]\n   511\t    (when-not (and (map? result) (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result)))\n   512\t      (throw (ex-info (str \"publish-index! stream append failed: \"\n   513\t                           (pr-str result))\n   514\t                      {:stream stream, :result result, :payload payload})))\n   515\t    result))\n   516\t\n   517\t\n   518\t(defn publish-index!\n   519\t  \"The transactor entry point, agent-side (docs/design/dao.jing.md,\n   520\t   Publication from an agent).\n   521\t\n   522\t   1. Snapshots the agent's local stream (snapshot-datoms) — fully, before\n   523\t      any publication append. Atomic transaction records are flattened here.\n   524\t   2. Builds the four covered indexes into a temporary recording content\n   525\t      handle through dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage, so addresses are\n   526\t      minted by jing/materialize! and equal blobs deduplicate in\n   527\t      children-before-parent first-insertion order.\n   528\t   3. Appends every unique node blob in that recorded order, then the\n   529\t      manifest, all to exactly the intake stream selected from the pool.\n   530\t   4. Returns {:manifest-address (jing/segment-key manifest) :manifest\n   531\t      manifest}.\n   532\t\n   533\t   The manifest address is derived from the manifest alone and never depends\n   534\t   on which intake stream carried it. A partial immutable prefix on a full\n   535\t   intake stream is acceptable and retry-safe: the manifest is always\n   536\t   appended last, so a :full failure can only have left node blobs.\n   537\t\n   538\t   Success acknowledges that every payload was appended to the selected\n   539\t   intake stream. It does not acknowledge that an asynchronous DaoJing\n   540\t   observer has materialized those payloads yet. Because the build starts at\n   541\t   the retained history's origin and reconstructs complete indexes,\n   542\t   local-stream must be on a complete-retention transport\n   543\t   (dao.stream.memory-log); the snapshot is complete before anything is\n   544\t   emitted.\n   545\t\n   546\t   Usage:\n   547\t     (publish-index! local-stream intake-pool)\n   548\t     (publish-index! local-stream intake-pool\n   549\t                     {:branching-factor n, :select-stream f})\n   550\t\n   551\t   opts: {:branching-factor n — max keys per node, at least 2 (default 512)\n   552\t          :select-stream f   — receives the pool and returns the intake\n   553\t                               stream to append to (default first)}\"\n   554\t  ([local-stream intake-pool] (publish-index! local-stream intake-pool nil))\n   555\t  ([local-stream intake-pool opts]\n   556\t   (let [branching (validate-branching! (:branching-factor opts 512))\n   557\t         intake (select-intake-stream! intake-pool (:select-stream opts first))\n   558\t         datoms (snapshot-datoms local-stream)\n   559\t         handle (recording-content-handle)\n   560\t         storage (bts/kv-storage handle {:branching-factor branching})\n   561\t         build-tree\n   562\t         (fn [cmp]\n   563\t           (bt/from-sequential cmp datoms {:branching-factor branching}))\n   564\t         trees {:eavt (build-tree eavt-cmp),\n   565\t                :aevt (build-tree aevt-cmp),\n   566\t                :avet (build-tree avet-cmp),\n   567\t                :vaet (build-tree vaet-cmp)}\n   568\t         root-addr (fn [tree]\n   569\t                     ;; an empty index has no root node; nil is the\n   570\t                     ;; explicit\n   571\t                     ;; \"nothing here\" (walk of nil => ())\n   572\t                     (when (seq datoms) (bt/store-tree tree storage)))\n   573\t         manifest {:indexes {:eavt (root-addr (:eavt trees)),\n   574\t                             :aevt (root-addr (:aevt trees)),\n   575\t                             :avet (root-addr (:avet trees)),\n   576\t                             :vaet (root-addr (:vaet trees))},\n   577\t                   ;; Covered indexes are sets. Their count must describe\n   578\t                   ;; the distinct tuples actually stored, not duplicate\n   579\t                   ;; occurrences in the source stream.\n   580\t                   :count (bt/count (:eavt trees)),\n   581\t                   :branching-factor branching}]\n   582\t     (doseq [[_ payload] (:order @(:state handle))] (append-ok! intake payload))\n   583\t     (append-ok! intake manifest)\n   584\t     {:manifest-address (jing/segment-key manifest), :manifest manifest})))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc'","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream.observer\n     2\t  \"Observer of one DaoStream medium: attachment, cursor, gap accounting, and\n     3\t   the coordination loop that feeds a consumer one batch at a time.\n     4\t\n     5\t   The medium exists independently of any consumer. Host composition supplies\n     6\t   a unary attach capability and a portable DaoStream descriptor; `attach`\n     7\t   calls that capability exactly once, validates the returned handle against\n     8\t   the reader surface, and mints the observer's cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`\n     9\t   directly through `dao.stream`. The observer never creates, appends to,\n    10\t   or closes the medium, and its complete initial state is\n    11\t   `{:stream handle :cursor cursor :ingress-gaps 0}`.\n    12\t\n    13\t   This namespace requires only `dao.stream` and `dao.stream.observe`,\n    14\t   whose single `step` it loops over. It cannot depend on a ring buffer, a\n    15\t   WebSocket adapter, a resolver representation, a transport key or state, or\n    16\t   any consumer's namespace: transport-specific composition constructs the\n    17\t   unary attacher outside this boundary, for example by partially applying a\n    18\t   dispatch table or a transport resolver. The consumer is opaque here — a\n    19\t   `yin.vm` evaluator, a macro expander, a `dao.space` indexer, or anything\n    20\t   else that can be asked whether it is ready, handed a batch, and run.\n    21\t\n    22\t   A `gap` is recoverable here and only here in the generic machinery. A batch\n    23\t   the observer never saw is advanced past at the recovery cursor and counted,\n    24\t   and observation continues with the next retained batch; silently pretending\n    25\t   the medium was contiguous hides missing data. What a host does with a gap\n    26\t   count is host policy — the REPL shell, for one, reports the loss and\n    27\t   refuses further evaluation until reset — and that policy lives above this\n    28\t   boundary. Terminal read outcomes are errors.\n    29\t\n    30\t   Exports exactly `attach`, `observe-next`, and `run-on-stream`.\"\n    31\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    32\t            [dao.stream.observe :as observe]))\n    33\t\n    34\t\n    35\t;; =============================================================================\n    36\t;; Attachment\n    37\t;; =============================================================================\n    38\t\n    39\t(defn attach\n    40\t  \"Attach to an existing medium and return the initial observer state\n    41\t   `{:stream handle :cursor cursor :ingress-gaps 0}`, or throw.\n    42\t\n    43\t   `attach!` is a unary attachment capability: `(attach! descriptor)`. It is\n    44\t   called exactly once and there is no fallback to stream creation. A\n    45\t   non-`ok` attachment or cursor outcome throws `ex-info` preserving the\n    46\t   original `:dao.stream/outcome`. A handle without the reader surface is a\n    47\t   host assembly error reported with the descriptor and the handle's declared\n    48\t   surfaces, without inventing a DaoStream outcome. Attachment cleanup stays\n    49\t   with the host: this function closes nothing, and a failure leaves no\n    50\t   partial observer behind.\"\n    51\t  [attach! descriptor]\n    52\t  (let [result (attach! descriptor)\n    53\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n    54\t    (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n    55\t      (throw (ex-info \"Stream attachment failed\"\n    56\t                      {:dao.stream/outcome outcome,\n    57\t                       :descriptor descriptor,\n    58\t                       :result result}))\n    59\t      (let [handle (:dao.stream/handle result)]\n    60\t        (if-not (stream/reader? handle)\n    61\t          (throw (ex-info \"Attached handle declares no reader surface\"\n    62\t                          {:descriptor descriptor,\n    63\t                           :surfaces (stream/declared-surfaces handle)}))\n    64\t          (let [minted (stream/cursor handle stream/anchor-oldest)\n    65\t                mint-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome minted)]\n    66\t            (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok mint-outcome)\n    67\t              (throw (ex-info \"Observer cursor could not be minted\"\n    68\t                              {:dao.stream/outcome mint-outcome,\n    69\t                               :descriptor descriptor,\n    70\t                               :result minted}))\n    71\t              {:stream handle,\n    72\t               :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor minted),\n    73\t               :ingress-gaps 0})))))))\n    74\t\n    75\t\n    76\t;; =============================================================================\n    77\t;; Observation\n    78\t;; =============================================================================\n    79\t\n    80\t(defn- terminal\n    81\t  \"Throw, preserving the original read outcome and the observer's own state.\"\n    82\t  [message outcome observer]\n    83\t  (throw (ex-info message\n    84\t                  {:dao.stream/outcome outcome,\n    85\t                   :cursor (:cursor observer),\n    86\t                   :ingress-gaps (:ingress-gaps observer)})))\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t(def ^:private uncontinuable-read-outcomes\n    90\t  \"The contract's read outcomes this observer cannot continue from.\"\n    91\t  #{:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\n    92\t    :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n    93\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\t(defn- answered-outcome\n    97\t  \"The outcome the transport actually answered with.\n    98\t\n    99\t   `observe/step` classifies an answer outside the contract as\n   100\t   `transport-error` and retains the raw answer, so the observer reports what\n   101\t   it was told rather than the classification.\"\n   102\t  [read]\n   103\t  (if (contains? read :dao.stream/answer)\n   104\t    (:dao.stream/outcome (:dao.stream/answer read))\n   105\t    (:dao.stream/outcome read)))\n   106\t\n   107\t\n   108\t(defn- observation-terminal\n   109\t  \"This observer's policy for a step defect: throw, naming the outcome the\n   110\t   transport answered with.\"\n   111\t  [observed observer]\n   112\t  (let [outcome (answered-outcome (:read observed))]\n   113\t    (if (contains? uncontinuable-read-outcomes outcome)\n   114\t      (terminal \"Observation cannot continue from this cursor\"\n   115\t                outcome\n   116\t                observer)\n   117\t      (terminal \"Unexpected DaoStream outcome while observing the medium\"\n   118\t                outcome\n   119\t                observer))))\n   120\t\n   121\t\n   122\t(defn- adopt-gap\n   123\t  \"This observer's gap policy: commit the recovery cursor and count the loss.\"\n   124\t  [observer observed]\n   125\t  (-> observer\n   126\t      (assoc :cursor (:recovery observed))\n   127\t      (update :ingress-gaps (fnil inc 0))))\n   128\t\n   129\t\n   130\t(defn observe-next\n   131\t  \"Observe one batch from the attached medium.\n   132\t\n   133\t   Returns `{:status :ok :batch batch :observer observer'}` on `ok`, where the\n   134\t   successor state carries the exact returned cursor; `{:status :blocked\n   135\t   :observer observer}` and `{:status :end :observer observer}`, which retain\n   136\t   the cursor; or `{:status :gap :observer observer'}`, where the successor\n   137\t   carries the recovery cursor and one more counted `:ingress-gaps`. Terminal\n   138\t   outcomes (`cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor`, `transport-error`) and\n   139\t   unexpected ones throw with the original outcome preserved.\"\n   140\t  [observer]\n   141\t  (let [observed (observe/step (:stream observer)\n   142\t                               (:cursor observer)\n   143\t                               (fn [_value] {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))]\n   144\t    (case (:status observed)\n   145\t      :advance {:status :ok,\n   146\t                :batch (:dao.stream/value (:read observed)),\n   147\t                :observer (assoc observer :cursor (:cursor observed))}\n   148\t\n   149\t      :retry {:status :blocked, :observer observer}\n   150\t      :ended {:status :end, :observer observer}\n   151\t      :gap {:status :gap, :observer (adopt-gap observer observed)}\n   152\t\n   153\t      (observation-terminal observed observer))))\n   154\t\n   155\t\n   156\t;; =============================================================================\n   157\t;; Coordination\n   158\t;; =============================================================================\n   159\t\n   160\t(defn run-on-stream\n   161\t  \"Coordinate one `{:observer observer :consumer consumer}` session over the\n   162\t   attached medium, returning the updated session.\n   163\t\n   164\t   The consumer is any value the three supplied functions agree on. `ready?`\n   165\t   decides when it may accept another batch, `load` hands it one observed\n   166\t   batch and returns the successor consumer, and `run` lets it do whatever\n   167\t   work a loaded batch implies — evaluate, expand, index, forward — and\n   168\t   returns the successor. This is coordination, not interpretation: no field\n   169\t   of the consumer is inspected, so any consumer shape can be driven.\n   170\t\n   171\t   A consumer that is not ready runs first, and observation follows only if\n   172\t   it becomes ready. A ready consumer observes one batch; `gap` commits the\n   173\t   recovery cursor and continues; `blocked` and `end` return the session.\n   174\t   After a batch loads and runs, a consumer that is ready again observes the\n   175\t   next batch, and one that is not returns the session without another read.\n   176\t\n   177\t   A `load` that throws propagates before any successor session is published:\n   178\t   the caller retains the previous observer cursor and the same batch is\n   179\t   re-read on the next call. A consumer whose input can be bad should\n   180\t   therefore return that as data from `load` rather than throw, and reserve\n   181\t   the throw for its own defects.\"\n   182\t  [session ready? load run]\n   183\t  (loop [{:keys [observer consumer]} session]\n   184\t    (if-not (ready? consumer)\n   185\t      (let [consumer' (run consumer)]\n   186\t        (if (ready? consumer')\n   187\t          (recur {:observer observer, :consumer consumer'})\n   188\t          {:observer observer, :consumer consumer'}))\n   189\t      ;; The load is the step's effect, so the cursor advances only once it has\n   190\t      ;; returned: a throwing load propagates before any successor exists.\n   191\t      (let [observed (observe/step (:stream observer)\n   192\t                                   (:cursor observer)\n   193\t                                   (fn [batch]\n   194\t                                     {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok,\n   195\t                                      :dao.stream.observer/loaded\n   196\t                                      (load consumer batch)}))]\n   197\t        (case (:status observed)\n   198\t          :advance (let [consumer' (run (:dao.stream.observer/loaded\n   199\t                                          (:effect observed)))\n   200\t                         observer' (assoc observer :cursor (:cursor observed))]\n   201\t                     (if (ready? consumer')\n   202\t                       (recur {:observer observer', :consumer consumer'})\n   203\t                       {:observer observer', :consumer consumer'}))\n   204\t          :gap (recur {:observer (adopt-gap observer observed),\n   205\t                       :consumer consumer})\n   206\t          ;; :retry (blocked) and :ended retain the cursor and end the round.\n   207\t          (:retry :ended) {:observer observer, :consumer consumer}\n   208\t          (observation-terminal observed observer))))))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.observe\n     2\t  \"One observation step over a DaoStream reader: read a value, act on it, and\n     3\t   advance the cursor only if the act succeeded.\n     4\t\n     5\t   `step` owns no scheduler, loop, state, callback or policy.  It classifies\n     6\t   every outcome the contract declares as data and hands the caller what it\n     7\t   needs to decide: the successor cursor on progress, the recovery cursor on a\n     8\t   gap, the raw answer on a defect.  What to do about a gap, whether a defect\n     9\t   is fatal, how many values to take per turn, and what state to carry between\n    10\t   turns all belong to the caller.\n    11\t\n    12\t   The cursor advances in exactly one place -- after the effect has answered\n    13\t   `ok` -- so effect-before-commit is a property of this seam rather than a\n    14\t   rule each caller keeps.  An effect that throws propagates before any\n    15\t   successor exists, which gives a throwing effect the same guarantee for\n    16\t   free.\n    17\t\n    18\t   Callers are `dao.stream.forward` (effect: append to another stream),\n    19\t   `dao.stream.observer` (effect: load a program into a VM) and\n    20\t   `dao.jing` (effect: materialize content).  Each adds its own loop and its\n    21\t   own policy above this step.\n    22\t\n    23\t   The law every interpreter here obeys is that an element's cursor advances\n    24\t   exactly when its disposition has been durably recorded.  This step serves\n    25\t   the interpreters whose disposition is an external, refusable effect -- an\n    26\t   append, a load, a materialization -- where that means advancing only after\n    27\t   the effect answered ok.  Interpreters whose disposition is a local, total\n    28\t   state transition keep their own read loops: `dao.stream.rpc/poll!`,\n    29\t   `dao.stream.apply/serve-once!` and `dao.runtime`'s wait set thread\n    30\t   whole caller state under a budget with terminal short-circuits, and for\n    31\t   them advancing first and advancing after the commit are the same fact,\n    32\t   because no window exists in which one happened and the other did not.\n    33\t   `serve-once!` is the proof that this is one law and not two families: it\n    34\t   advances after delivery for a correlatable request, on the diagnostic for\n    35\t   an uncorrelatable one, and on terminal loss for an undeliverable response.\n    36\t\n    37\t   There is no ordering parameter here and none is needed.  A consumer that\n    38\t   wants an element consumed even when its interpretation is defective writes\n    39\t   a *total* effect -- one that classifies every element as data and so always\n    40\t   answers ok -- and gets consume-exactly-once through this same step.\"\n    41\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\t(defn- valid-or-transport-error\n    45\t  \"Fold a defective host answer into the contract's outcome algebra.\n    46\t\n    47\t   A conforming operation already returns one of the contract outcomes; this\n    48\t   keeps the step's branch total when a composition supplies a bad handle or\n    49\t   an effect answers with something that is not an outcome map.\"\n    50\t  [operation result]\n    51\t  (if (stream/valid-outcome? operation result)\n    52\t    result\n    53\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n    54\t     :dao.stream/error :dao.stream/invalid-operation-result\n    55\t     :dao.stream/answer result}))\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(defn step\n    59\t  \"Read one value from `source` at `cursor` and run `effect` on it.\n    60\t\n    61\t   `effect` is `(fn [value] -> outcome-map)` answering with the writer outcome\n    62\t   set: `ok`, `full` (\\\"not yet\\\"), `invalid-value`, `closed`, or\n    63\t   `transport-error`.  Any other answer is classified as `transport-error`\n    64\t   with the raw answer retained.  An effect that throws propagates.\n    65\t\n    66\t   Returns a map carrying `:cursor` and `:read` (the raw read answer) in every\n    67\t   case:\n    68\t\n    69\t   | status     | when                                    | cursor    | also         |\n    70\t   | ---------- | --------------------------------------- | --------- | ------------ |\n    71\t   | `:advance` | read ok, effect ok                      | successor | `:effect`    |\n    72\t   | `:retry`   | read blocked, or effect full            | unchanged | `:outcome`   |\n    73\t   | `:ended`   | read end                                | unchanged | `:outcome`   |\n    74\t   | `:gap`     | read gap                                | unchanged | `:recovery`  |\n    75\t   | `:defect`  | read cursor-mismatch, invalid-cursor,\n    76\t                  transport-error, or malformed                | unchanged | `:outcome`   |\n    77\t   | `:failed`  | effect invalid-value, closed,\n    78\t                  transport-error, or malformed                | unchanged | `:outcome`, `:effect` |\n    79\t\n    80\t   A `:retry` from the effect also carries `:effect`.  The cursor is the\n    81\t   successor on `:advance` and unchanged on every other status.\"\n    82\t  [source cursor effect]\n    83\t  (let [read-result (valid-or-transport-error :next (stream/next source cursor))\n    84\t        read-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome read-result)]\n    85\t    (case read-outcome\n    86\t      :dao.stream/ok\n    87\t      (let [effect-result (valid-or-transport-error\n    88\t                            :append!\n    89\t                            (effect (:dao.stream/value read-result)))\n    90\t            effect-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome effect-result)]\n    91\t        (case effect-outcome\n    92\t          :dao.stream/ok {:status :advance\n    93\t                          :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result)\n    94\t                          :read read-result\n    95\t                          :effect effect-result}\n    96\t          :dao.stream/full {:status :retry\n    97\t                            :cursor cursor\n    98\t                            :outcome effect-outcome\n    99\t                            :read read-result\n   100\t                            :effect effect-result}\n   101\t          {:status :failed\n   102\t           :cursor cursor\n   103\t           :outcome effect-outcome\n   104\t           :read read-result\n   105\t           :effect effect-result}))\n   106\t\n   107\t      :dao.stream/blocked {:status :retry\n   108\t                           :cursor cursor\n   109\t                           :outcome read-outcome\n   110\t                           :read read-result}\n   111\t\n   112\t      :dao.stream/end {:status :ended\n   113\t                       :cursor cursor\n   114\t                       :outcome read-outcome\n   115\t                       :read read-result}\n   116\t\n   117\t      :dao.stream/gap {:status :gap\n   118\t                       :cursor cursor\n   119\t                       :outcome read-outcome\n   120\t                       :recovery (:dao.stream/cursor read-result)\n   121\t                       :read read-result}\n   122\t\n   123\t      {:status :defect\n   124\t       :cursor cursor\n   125\t       :outcome read-outcome\n   126\t       :read read-result})))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
