t            (ex-info\n    63\t              \"canonical d5 source element must be a 5-datom vector or a transaction record\"\n    64\t              {:element x})))))\n    65\t    elements))\n    66\t\n    67\t\n    68\t(defn- bound-datoms\n    69\t  \"Bound a d5 sequence to t <= as-of when an as-of is given.\"\n    70\t  [datoms as-of]\n    71\t  (if as-of\n    72\t    (filterv #(<= (index/compare-vals (index/datom-t %) as-of) 0) datoms)\n    73\t    datoms))\n    74\t\n   140\t;; =============================================================================\n   141\t\n   142\t(defn value?\n   143\t  \"True for the query values q/match/pull take as database inputs: a\n   144\t   relation value, a datom view, or an opened published index. Raw vectors\n   145\t   and raw maps answer false.\"\n   146\t  [x]\n   147\t  (and (map? x)\n   148\t       (or (contains? x :dao.space.query/relation)\n   149\t           (contains? x :dao.space.query/view)\n   150\t           (contains? x :dao.space.query/published))))\n   151\t\n   152\t\n   153\t(defn relation\n   154\t  \"An in-memory relation value. Its retained contents are an arbitrary,\n   155\t   mixed-dimensional tuple collection; arity never selects an\n   156\t   interpretation. This is the replacement for the direct raw-vector input.\"\n   157\t  [tuples]\n   158\t  {:dao.space.query/relation (vec tuples)})\n   159\t\n   160\t\n   161\t(defn entity-map-relation\n   162\t  \"An entity-map relation value: a bounded relation of entity maps,\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.md | sed -n '70,147p;356,457p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md | sed -n '142,181p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.md | sed -n '1,85p;240,355p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '741,835p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.md | sed -n '70,147p;356,457p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md | sed -n '142,181p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.md | sed -n '1,85p;240,355p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '741,835p'\"","aggregated_output":"    70\t## Three Boundaries\n    71\t\n    72\tDatomic's central strength is the strict separation of **Transactor**, **Storage**, and\n    73\t**Query** into *abstraction boundaries* — interfaces, not deployment tiers. They can be\n    74\tco-located in a single process with zero network overhead, or split across machines, without\n    75\tchanging the contracts. datom.world keeps that separation and maps it onto streams:\n    76\t\n    77\t- **Transactor (write + index)** — **decentralized.** Every agent appending to its own\n    78\t  `dao.stream` is its own transactor: it writes atomic transaction records to its local\n    79\t  stream, with no global contention and no commit step. It also carries the transactor's\n    80\t  other Datomic duty — building the covered indexes over its own datoms — via the\n    81\t  [`dao.space.index`](dao.space.index.md) library: `publish-index!` snapshots the stream,\n    82\t  builds the four B-tree indexes, and appends them (node blobs, then the manifest) to an\n    83\t  intake stream that a DaoJing observer materializes into content storage.\n    84\t- **Storage** — **[`dao.jing`](dao.jing.md).** The content-addressed store where indexes\n    85\t  live persistently. It observes an explicit pool of intake streams and materializes each\n    86\t  opaque payload insert-if-absent under its content address (`:segment/sha256-<hash>`).\n    87\t  It holds the B-tree node blobs and the top-level manifests; it maintains no membership,\n    88\t  no mutable roots, and no CAS surface, and it does **not** match or query.\n    89\t- **Query (read)** — **the [`dao.space.query`](#the-query-library) library any interpreter\n    90\t  embeds.** It reads from two layers — in-process relation/entity-map sources, and\n    91\t  published sources (a content store plus a manifest address) resolved through the\n    92\t  covered-index realization — and runs pattern matching, Datalog, and pull. Pure,\n    93\t  in-process, per-interpreter — Datomic's Peer model, as a library rather than a\n    94\t  service.\n    95\t\n    96\t**`dao.space` is not one of the three boundaries; it is the coordination surface that spans\n    97\tall three.** The transactor writes and indexes; storage persists; the query matches. The\n    98\ttuple space is the behavior that emerges when agents exercise all three faculties — writing\n    99\tto their streams, indexing for themselves and others, and matching over the result. This\n   100\tdocument specifies that tuple space. Storage is specified separately in\n   101\t[`dao.jing.md`](dao.jing.md).\n   102\t\n   103\t## What Makes It a Tuple Space\n   104\t\n   105\tBoth moves the intro defines — associative matching and generative communication — are\n   106\tbehaviors of the two libraries agents use, **not** properties of storage. A store that\n   107\tmatched would collapse interpretation into storage, which datom.world's invariants forbid.\n   108\tSo the \"space-ness\" lives above `dao.jing`, in the composition of index and query:\n   109\t\n   110\t- `dao.stream` — each agent's append-only log (the write path).\n   111\t- `dao.space.index` — snapshots the stream's datoms and builds the covered indexes,\n   112\t  enqueuing them through a DaoJing intake pool when `publish-index!` runs.\n   113\t- `dao.space.query` — matches over published sources and in-process tuple sources,\n   114\t  associatively.\n   115\t- Agents coordinate by what is there (stigmergy), never by addressing each other.\n   116\t\n   117\tA tuple space is therefore not an artifact you instantiate; it is the behavior that appears\n   118\twhen agents index and match over shared storage. `dao.stream` is where an agent writes;\n   119\t`dao.jing` is where indexes persist; `dao.space` is what agents *do* across both.\n   120\t\n   121\t### Tuples and the Datom Convention\n   122\t\n   123\tBecause the tuple space lives in the interpreter, DaoSpace accepts immutable\n   124\ttuples of any finite dimension. A tuple's arity is structure, never an implicit\n   125\tinterpretation. `dao.space.query` matches arbitrary and mixed dimensions with\n   126\texact arity by default; explicit rest syntax requests prefix matching.\n   127\t\n   128\tThe d5 datom `(e a v t m)` remains the canonical persistent fact protocol. An\n   129\texplicit datom view gives those positions entity, attribute, value,\n   130\ttransaction, and metadata meaning and enables automatic covered indexes. A\n   131\tgeneric five-tuple does not become a datom merely because its count is five.\n   132\t\n   133\t`dao.jing` is even less restrictive: it materializes any opaque payload an\n   134\tintake stream carries. It is an interpretation of DaoStream as\n   135\tcontent-addressed key/value storage, but it assigns no domain semantics. Any\n   136\tpoint in Datom.world's database moduli space may use DaoJing for storage; the\n   137\tconsumer above storage decides whether a payload is a tuple, index, graph,\n   138\tmodel, or something else.\n   139\t\n   140\t## The Query Library\n   141\t\n   142\tPattern matching and Datalog are a **library** (`dao.space.query`) that any interpreter\n   143\tembeds and runs against content stores and in-process sources. It is pure: fold a source\n   144\tinto an index, answer. It owns no durable state and never writes.\n   145\t\n   146\tThe library reads from **two layers**, reflecting where its tuple inputs live:\n   147\t\n   356\t## The Write Path\n   357\t\n   358\tThe read side is matching; the write side is how datoms get into the shared medium. A datom\n   359\tenters by being appended to a writer-owned `dao.stream` — an append-only log the writer owns\n   360\tand never edits in place. Opening a log is what makes a writer a participant: it attaches a\n   361\tfeeding stream to the space; closing it detaches. This is the mechanics behind the\n   362\tgenerative-communication move described in the intro (deposit by appending, name no\n   363\trecipient) and behind the decentralized Transactor of [Three Boundaries](#three-boundaries).\n   364\t\n   365\t### Intake is explicit topology, not identity\n   366\t\n   367\tTwo explicit topologies must stay distinct, because they sit on different sides of the\n   368\tstorage boundary (`dao.jing.md`, *Physical intake versus semantic composition*):\n   369\t\n   370\t- **DaoJing intake pools** — the streams a DaoJing observer materializes into content\n   371\t  storage. Physical ingestion topology.\n   372\t- **Query pools** — the published index descriptors a reader currently opens. Semantic composition:\n   373\t  an explicit collection of DaoStream descriptors.\n   374\t\n   375\tPools may change at runtime by producing a new collection, while each published manifest\n   376\tand its materialized history persist independently. Storage, [`dao.jing`](dao.jing.md),\n   377\tremains the dumb materialization; it never interprets or registers pool membership.\n   378\t\n   379\tBecause each writer owns a single-writer log, two agents never write the same stream. If\n   380\t1,000 agents want to send messages to one recipient, they append to 1,000 distinct\n   381\tsingle-writer streams, and the recipient merges them on the read side — no shared write\n   382\tsurface, no contention.\n   383\t\n   384\t### Indexing is the writer's duty\n   385\t\n   386\tThe write path does not end at the append. In Datomic the transactor also builds the covered\n   387\tindexes and saves them to storage; here that duty is decentralized with the rest of the\n   388\tTransactor. Indexing has two stages, mirroring Datomic's memory-index → disk-index pipeline\n   389\tbut without a central transactor process:\n   390\t\n   391\t1. **Append** — the agent's writes land in its own local `dao.stream`\n   392\t   memory-log as atomic transaction records (see\n   393\t   [`dao.space.transactor`](dao.space.transactor.md)). The local stream is\n   394\t   authoritative for the logical stream's process lifetime — the watermark and\n   395\t   the published indexes are derived from it — but it is not durable: a\n   396\t   restarted process sees a new, empty logical stream with a new identity.\n   397\t   **The durable record is what publication puts in `dao.jing`**, and\n   398\t   un-published writes are not durable — which was equally true before the\n   399\t   v2 migration, when local streams were in-memory ring buffers. The\n   400\t   pipeline mirrors Datomic's memory-index → disk-index, and a memory index\n   401\t   is not durable either. A durable *stream* transport is not the fix: it\n   402\t   would put a second durable record beside the content store, which is\n   403\t   exactly the `dao.stream`/`dao.jing` unification\n   404\t   [`dao.space.query.md`](dao.space.query.md)'s *Decisions* ruled out (\"the\n   405\t   source stays a convention layered over the dumb content store … unifying\n   406\t   them would undo that\"). No storage handle is touched at append.\n   407\t2. **Publish** — when the agent runs\n   408\t   [`dao.space.index/publish-index!`](dao.space.index.md), the local stream is snapshotted,\n   409\t   the four covered indexes are built as immutable, content-addressed `dao.data.btree`\n   410\t   segments, and the node blobs plus the manifest are appended to one intake stream\n   411\t   selected from an explicit pool. A DaoJing observer materializes them into content\n   412\t   storage; other agents consume the persisted covered indexes by naming the manifest\n   413\t   address with a published source. The datoms themselves were already readable by anyone\n   414\t   who folds the published manifest; publication changes access cost, not visibility.\n   415\t\n   416\tPublishing is an acceleration, never a semantic change for `q`/`match` — readers answer\n   417\tidentically over a published manifest or a raw datom source. `publish-index!` returns the\n   418\tmanifest and its address and never mutates DaoJing directly; append success means enqueued,\n   419\tnot yet materialized. A partial immutable node prefix on a full intake stream is retry-safe\n   420\tbecause the manifest is always appended last. See `dao.space.index.md`, *The\n   421\tagent-transactor loop*.\n   422\t\n   423\t### Fault Tolerance (Crash-Only Semantics)\n   424\t\n   425\tThe write path is crash-only because it is built from atomic appends and\n   426\timmutable publications — not because anything local is durable. Two failure\n   427\tscopes behave differently, and the difference is the point:\n   428\t\n   429\t- **Atomicity, in both scopes:** every write is one atomic transaction\n   430\t  record through one local append (`dao.space.transactor`), and every\n   431\t  publication is an immutable, content-addressed manifest appended after\n   432\t  its node blobs. A failure can leave a prefix, never a torn transaction or\n   433\t  a partial manifest.\n   434\t- **A writer task stops; the process lives.** Its handles remain owned and\n   435\t  readable: a reader reaching the stopped writer's local-stream tail is\n   436\t  told `:dao.stream/blocked` and waits for new data rather than failing —\n   437\t  the same for a DaoJing observer over an intake stream. Retrying an\n   438\t  interrupted publication is idempotent while the memory-log is still\n   439\t  available: content addressing deduplicates a re-emitted prefix, and the\n   440\t  manifest is appended last.\n   441\t- **The process fails.** The local stream is a process-lifetime\n   442\t  `dao.stream` memory-log and the intake streams are in-memory handles,\n   443\t  so un-published local contents — and intake payloads a DaoJing observer\n   444\t  has not yet materialized — may be lost; nothing tails a dead handle.\n   445\t  Recovery has only what DaoJing has fully materialized: a restarted\n   446\t  process sees a new, empty local logical stream with a new identity, and\n   447\t  the agent continues from the manifest addresses it (or its\n   448\t  configuration) names. Durability begins at publication, never before\n   449\t  (see *The Write Path* above, and `dao.space.transactor.md`, *Where\n   450\t  durability lives*).\n   451\t\n   452\tThe causality-carrying checkpoint named in `dao.space.transactor.md`'s\n   453\t*Open items* is not relief for the second scope: it is a performance\n   454\toptimisation that would reduce the O(history) causality replay `create!`\n   455\tpays on open, and it adds no durability — un-published history would\n   456\tremain un-recoverable, checkpoint or not.\n   457\t\n   142\t## Where durability lives\n   143\t\n   144\tThe local stream is the **authoritative record for the logical stream's\n   145\tlifetime** — the watermark and every published index are derived from it —\n   146\tand it is **not durable**: `memory-log` is process-lifetime, and a restarted\n   147\tprocess sees a new, empty logical stream with a new identity. That is the\n   148\tcorrect shape, not a shortfall:\n   149\t\n   150\t- **Stage 1, append** — the write lands in the agent's local stream and no\n   151\t  storage handle is touched.\n   152\t- **Stage 2, publish** — the covered indexes and manifest are appended to an\n   153\t  intake stream and a DaoJing observer materializes them into content\n   154\t  storage.\n   155\t\n   156\t**The durable record is what publication puts in `dao.jing`.** Un-published\n   157\twrites are not durable, and never were — v1's local streams were in-memory\n   158\tring buffers, so this was equally true before the migration; the design\n   159\tmerely claimed otherwise. The pipeline mirrors Datomic's memory-index →\n   160\tdisk-index, and a memory index is not durable either. A durable *stream*\n   161\ttransport is not the answer and should not be proposed as the fix: it would\n   162\tput a second durable record beside the content store, which is exactly the\n   163\t`dao.stream`/`dao.jing` unification `dao.space.query.md`'s *Decisions*\n   164\talready ruled out.\n   165\t\n   166\t## Open items\n   167\t\n   168\t- **The O(history) watermark replay.** `create!` derives `t` by reading the\n   169\t  whole retained history (`index/snapshot-datoms`), and `publish!` rebuilds\n   170\t  the indexes from the origin — both correct today *because the transport\n   171\t  cannot evict*. The eventual relief keeps one truth — the log carries its\n   172\t  own checkpoint, from which the watermark and incremental indexes resume —\n   173\t  rather than adding a second stored counter that can disagree with the\n   174\t  record. Not designed here.\n     1\t# dao.space.index — The Transactor-Side Indexing Library\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: implemented. The mechanism (owner-built, content-addressed B-Tree\n     4\tsegments) shipped 2026-07-10 and runs today on `dao.data.btree`; this document\n     5\trecords why the library exists, what it owns, its public surface, and the\n     6\tboundary between it and the query library. The executable contract is\n     7\t`test/dao/space/index_test.cljc`.\n     8\t\n     9\t**Related documents:**\n    10\t- `docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md` — the write path that calls\n    11\t  `publish-index!`; the local-stream wiring requirement lives there and here\n    12\t- `docs/design/dao.space.query.md` — the reader-side consumer of the\n    13\t  realization this library owns\n    14\t- `docs/design/dao.space.md` — the tuple space; *Three Boundaries* maps\n    15\t  Transactor/Storage/Query onto streams\n    16\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.md` — the storage boundary; publication through an\n    17\t  explicit intake pool\n    18\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` — the distributed backend the published blobs\n    19\t  can be stored into\n    20\t- `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` — the cross-platform tree and its storage\n    21\t  adapter\n    22\t- `docs/datomic.md` — the architecture the analogy below leans on\n    23\t\n    24\t## Why a separate library\n    25\t\n    26\tIn Datomic, the **transactor** builds the covered indexes and saves them to\n    27\tstorage; **peers** pull segments and answer queries. datom.world decentralizes\n    28\tthe transactor: every agent appending to its own `dao.stream` is its own\n    29\ttransactor (`dao.space.md`, *Three Boundaries*) — so every agent also owns the\n    30\ttransactor's other duty, **indexing its own datoms**. That duty needs a\n    31\tlibrary the same way querying does:\n    32\t\n    33\t- `dao.space.index` — what a stream owner runs: snapshot the local stream,\n    34\t  build the covered indexes, and enqueue them as immutable content-addressed\n    35\t  segments plus a manifest through a DaoJing intake stream. The write-side\n    36\t  counterpart of the pair.\n    37\t- `dao.space.query` — the embeddable Peer: open bounded source coordinates,\n    38\t  match, run Datalog, and pull. The transportable read coordinate belongs to\n    39\t  `dao.space.index`; query's `open-published!` opens it, consuming only its\n    40\t  logical d5 rows and covered sets.\n    41\t\n    42\tThe boundary is strict: **query never writes; index owns the realization both\n    43\tsides share.** It is the same move Datomic makes between transactor and peer —\n    44\tone party materializes, many parties consume — except here \"the transactor\"\n    45\tis not a process but a library duty every agent carries.\n    46\t\n    47\tCovered indexes are physical access paths within the DaoSpace tuple-space\n    48\tpoint. They change lookup cost, not tuple meaning. Replacing or supplementing\n    49\tthem with explicitly requested positional indexes would still be DaoSpace as\n    50\tlong as the logical operation remains exact associative tuple matching.\n    51\t\n    52\t## What the library owns\n    53\t\n    54\tOne namespace, `src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc`. Everything below is the index\n    55\t*realization*: the shared vocabulary a builder and a reader must agree on.\n    56\t\n    57\t- **Sort orders** — `eavt-cmp` / `aevt-cmp` / `avet-cmp` / `vaet-cmp` over\n    58\t  heterogeneous values (`compare-vals`: type-ranked, nil-first). The\n    59\t  comparators also serve query-only db-values, whose ids may be caller-chosen;\n    60\t  persisted local d5 datoms are stricter: non-negative integer e/t, integer\n    61\t  m, and namespaced keyword a. The library also owns the datom slot accessors\n    62\t  (`datom-e/a/v/t/m`) they read through. Covered indexes contain canonical\n    63\t  local d5 datoms only. Source scope belongs to the query interpreter and is\n    64\t  not appended to an indexed tuple.\n    65\t- **The in-memory index value** — `index-datoms` builds\n    66\t  `{:eavt :aevt :avet :vaet}` `dao.data.btree` sorted sets. `subseq-from`\n    67\t  delegates to `dao.data.btree/slice`: a log-n descent that, on a restored\n    68\t  tree, loads only the seek path plus the matching range. The implementation\n    69\t  is the same on JVM, ClojureScript, and ClojureDart.\n    70\t- **The snapshot** — `snapshot-datoms` reads an agent-local stream in full\n    71\t  through the dao.stream reader surface: mint an `:oldest` cursor, then\n    72\t  `next` to the tail. The local stream must be on a complete-retention\n    73\t  transport — a handle created by `dao.stream.memory-log/create!`; its\n    74\t  declared complete retention is why a fresh `:oldest` cursor is the origin,\n    75\t  so the read starts at the logical sequence's beginning and `gap` cannot\n    76\t  occur. `stream/reader?`/`stream/writer?` check *surfaces*, not retention —\n    77\t  they do not establish the completeness this read depends on. A stream\n    78\t  element is either one canonical datom vector `[e a v t m]` or one atomic\n    79\t  transaction record `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}`;\n    80\t  transaction records are validated and flattened into their datoms. Each\n    81\t  element is validated and flattened as it is read, so the first defect in\n    82\t  stream order is the one reported and no read happens past it. Every result\n    83\t  is validated with `stream/validate-outcome` before it is interpreted — a\n    84\t  malformed result (say, an `ok` carrying no cursor) throws instead of\n    85\t  recurring on a nil cursor. `blocked` (an open stream caught up) and `end`\n   240\t                       :dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity 4096}))])\n   241\t\n   242\t(def log (transactor/create! {:local-stream local\n   243\t                              :intake-pool intake-pool\n   244\t                              :name \"worker-7\"}))     ; one wrapper per local stream\n   245\t\n   246\t(transactor/append! log {:db/id id :work/claims task}) ; 1. deposit — one atomic\n   247\t;; ... more appends ...                                ;    transaction record\n   248\t(transactor/publish! log)                              ; 2. snapshot, build, enqueue\n   249\t```\n   250\t\n   251\tThe value's `append!` / `transact!` write exactly one atomic transaction\n   252\trecord to the local stream per call, so no reader observes a torn transaction.\n   253\tOn `create!` it reads the retained history from its origin and derives the\n   254\tnext `t` (0 for an empty history, else 1 + the maximum datom t); the local\n   255\tstream must therefore be on a complete-retention transport —\n   256\t`dao.stream.memory-log/create!`. `create!` validates reader/writer\n   257\t*surfaces*, never retention: a `stream/reader?`/`stream/writer?` check does\n   258\tnot establish complete retention, and supplying an evicting transport is a\n   259\thost-assembly defect (detectable, deliberately not checked — see\n   260\t`dao.space.transactor.md`, T18). One wrapper per local stream is a hard\n   261\tsingle-writer invariant. Calls through one wrapper serialize timestamp\n   262\tallocation and append on shared-memory hosts; two wrappers over the same\n   263\tstream still each derive the same `t` and write colliding records, which\n   264\tcannot be silently coordinated without shared mutable state. `publish!` delegates explicitly to\n   265\t`index/publish-index!`, passing the wrapper's local stream, intake pool, and\n   266\topts, and returning its `{:manifest-address ... :manifest ...}`.\n   267\t\n   268\tTwo lifecycle facts are deliberate:\n   269\t\n   270\t- Publication is an acceleration, never a semantic change — `q`/`match`\n   271\t  answer identically before and after (pinned by\n   272\t  `publish-index-snapshot-reads-local-stream-and-reads-back` and the\n   273\t  observer-materialization parity tests).\n   274\t- `query/open-published!` opens the published coordinate lazily (manifest\n   275\t  fetch plus restored sets) and defers the EAVT rows behind `query/rows`;\n   276\t  query consumes the restored sets directly for selective current reads,\n   277\t  forces the rows for rest-pattern and 4+-slot scans, and walks the restored\n   278\t  tree for unselective 3-fixed scans. Query does not dispatch on manifests,\n   279\t  pools, or B-tree segments. Publishing changes access cost, never\n   280\t  the datoms.\n   281\t\n   282\t## Dependency picture\n   283\t\n   284\t```\n   285\tdao.space.transactor  ──►  dao.space.index  ◄──  dao.space.query\n   286\t   (write path:          (realization:          (the Peer:\n   287\t    append!/transact!      B-tree values,          open published coordinate,\n   288\t    write transaction      publish-index!,         match, q, pull —\n   289\t    records; publish!      sort orders,            reads relations and views\n   290\t    delegates here)        node blobs)             or published manifests)\n   291\t                                │\n   292\t                                ▼\n   293\t                          dao.data.btree ◄── dao.data.btree.storage\n   294\t                          (the tree)            (IStorage over a content handle)\n   295\t                                │\n   296\t                                ▼\n   297\t                          dao.jing (content-store handle)\n   298\t```\n   299\t\n   300\t- `dao.space.query` requires `dao.space.index` for manifest reading and index\n   301\t  traversal. It does not define or persist a second index format.\n   302\t- `dao.space.transactor` requires `dao.space.index` for snapshotting,\n   303\t  validation, and publication. The write path does not depend on the Datalog\n   304\t  engine.\n   305\t- `dao.space.index` requires `dao.data.btree`,\n   306\t  `dao.data.btree.storage`, and `dao.jing`; it never requires\n   307\t  `dao.space.query`. No cycle is possible: realization below, interpretation\n   308\t  above.\n   309\t\n   310\tStorage stays dumb throughout (Decision 1 of `dao.space.query.md`, no new\n   311\tstorage protocol): everything\n   312\there is built from `dao.jing/materialize!` and `dao.jing/get` against strict\n   313\tsegment addresses. Storage never knows the segment blobs form an index, and\n   314\tthere is no mutable root for it to maintain.\n   315\t\n   316\t## Platform status\n   317\t\n   318\tBuild, the eager published read (`read-datoms`), lazy lower-level\n   319\trestoration, and range slicing are all\n   320\t**cross-platform**. `dao.data.btree` and `dao.data.btree.storage` are `.cljc`\n   321\timplementations shared by JVM, ClojureScript, and ClojureDart.\n   322\t\n   323\tThe eager published path uses `walk-index-datoms`, which understands the plain EDN node\n   324\tblobs using only `jing/get`. The separate lazy API uses\n   325\t`dao.data.btree/restore-tree` through\n   326\t`dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage`; traversal faults only the required nodes.\n   327\tThe manifest's `:count` and `:branching-factor` are threaded through\n   328\t`restore-tree` deliberately: count keeps O(1) `count` on restored trees\n   329\twithout faulting the graph, and the branching factor reaches every restored\n   330\tnode so mutation splits at the published thresholds. Tests in\n   331\t`test/dao/space/index_test.cljc` and `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc` pin\n   332\tboth contracts, including lazy point-lookup fetch counts for the\n   333\tlower-level restored tree and, in `query_test`, coordinate transport and\n   334\teager logical d5 reads through the opened published value.\n   335\t\n   336\t## Open items\n   337\t\n   338\t- **Segment GC** — superseded index segments accumulate forever.\n   339\t- **Published indexed snapshots and K-way merge** — queries currently consume\n   340\t  each published manifest through one opened coordinate at a time.\n   341\t- **Incremental indexing** — the natural next increment for long-lived agent\n   342\t  transactors: today an owner republishes wholesale from the full datom seq. A\n   343\t  future builder could retain the previous manifest, insert only the appended\n   344\t  datoms into restored B-trees, and store their changed paths. Nothing in the\n   345\t  node or manifest format requires that strategy; the current implementation\n   346\t  always performs a full rebuild. The design for this is\n   347\t  [`dao.space.index.as-observer.md`](./dao.space.index.as-observer.md): this\n   348\t  library driven batch by batch through `dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream`\n   349\t  over any medium, with `publish-index!` kept as the stateless special case.\n   350\t- **Async hydration** — remote reads over async backends use the hydration\n   351\t  adapter (`dao.data.btree.storage/hydration-storage`, `hydrate!`); the async\n   352\t  variants (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`) are deferred until an async\n   353\t  DaoJing backend exists.\n   741\t## 5. The expander as an observer\n   742\t\n   743\tThe expander is driven by `dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` with\n   744\tits state in the `:consumer` slot — the coordination inspects no field of\n   745\tthe consumer, so it drives an expander as readily as a VM:\n   746\t\n   747\t```\n   748\texpander = {:ctx ctx\n   749\t            :out writer        :out-staged  nil|datoms\n   750\t            :log writer|nil    :log-staged  nil|datoms\n   751\t            :forwarded 0       ; batches appended ok to :out since the last drain\n   752\t            :errors []}        ; expansion failures since the last drain\n   753\t\n   754\tready?       (fn [x] (and (nil? (:out-staged x)) (nil? (:log-staged x))))\n   755\tload         (fn [x batch]\n   756\t               (let [r (expand-batch batch (:ctx x))]\n   757\t                 (-> x (assoc :ctx (:ctx r))\n   758\t                       (assoc :out-staged (when (= :ok (:status r)) (:datoms r)))\n   759\t                       (assoc :log-staged (when (:log x) (:log r)))\n   760\t                       (cond-> (= :error (:status r)) (update :errors conj (:error r))))))\n   761\trun          flush                    ; below\n   762\tdrain-errors (fn [x] [(assoc x :errors [] :forwarded 0) {:errors (:errors x) :forwarded (:forwarded x)}])\n   763\t```\n   764\t\n   765\t`:errors` and `:forwarded` are **read-and-reset** by the driver through\n   766\t`drain-errors`, once per driver round, after `run-on-stream` returns and\n   767\tbefore the driver decides what to do next. Nothing else clears them; a\n   768\tdriver that never drains sees an ever-growing list, which is its bug, not a\n   769\tstuck expander (the cursor has moved on regardless).\n   770\t\n   771\t**Flush** delivers each staged payload to its own medium and clears *that*\n   772\tslot on `ok`, independently:\n   773\t\n   774\t+----------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   775\t| Outcome on a medium                          | Effect on its slot                                                                                                      |\n   776\t+==============================================+=========================================================================================================================+\n   777\t| `ok`                                         | slot ← nil; for `:out`, `:forwarded` += 1                                                                               |\n   778\t+----------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   779\t| `full`                                       | slot kept; expander stays not-ready; the same payload is retried next round. `full` may be permanent; retention is      |\n   780\t|                                              | correct, progress is the composition's to arrange                                                                       |\n   781\t+----------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   782\t| `closed`, `invalid-value`, `transport-error` | legitimate protocol outcomes that this forwarder cannot continue from: throw, preserving the original outcome and both  |\n   783\t|                                              | slots' state in the thrown value, so the composition decides                                                            |\n   784\t+----------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   785\t\n   786\tOnce a medium has answered `ok` its payload is never appended again by an\n   787\tordinary retry, so `out = ok, log = full` re-tries only the log. A batch\n   788\tthat expands with `:error` stages nothing for `program-out` and stages the\n   789\tevent for the log; the source cursor advances (the load returned), the\n   790\tfailure is on the log, and the driver drains it. Program and log are\n   791\tindependent media: this design offers *at-most-once per medium* and batch\n   792\torder per medium under ordinary retries, not atomic visibility across the\n   793\ttwo and not crash recovery; a composition that needs atomicity commits both\n   794\tto one `dao.space` transaction (§4.2).\n   795\t\n   796\t`run-on-stream`'s contract supplies the rest: a `full` leaves the expander\n   797\tnot-ready with the exact payload staged, so the next round retries the\n   798\tappend before observing again; `gap` on `program-in` is counted by the\n   799\tobserver and is the composition's policy, as for any observer. What remains\n   800\ta *throw* out of `load` is a forwarder defect (codec failure, index\n   801\tfailure); the cursor does not advance and the batch is re-read, which is\n   802\tthe correct behaviour for a bug and the wrong one for bad input — hence\n   803\tdecision 11.\n   804\t\n   805\t**Prerequisite on the observer (Phase 0).** `run-on-stream` today publishes\n   806\tits successor session only on return. If it forwards A, then B's load or run\n   807\tthrows, the caller still holds the pre-A session and a retry forwards A again\n   808\t(reproduced by the round-2 review against the current observer). This is a\n   809\tdefect of the generic coordination, not of this design — a VM loader that\n   810\tthrows on B after loading A has the same problem — and it is fixed there.\n   811\tThe fix **keeps the throw** and carries the partial session in the\n   812\texception's data: `(ex-info … {:session {:observer o' :consumer c'} …})`. A\n   813\treturn-shaped `{:observer :consumer :error}` was considered and rejected: every\n   814\texisting caller reasons \"a throw means the round failed\", and a value-shaped\n   815\terror would let an un-updated caller continue silently past a defect. With\n   816\tthe throw kept, recovery is opt-in: a caller that catches resumes from\n   817\t`:session`; one that does not keeps today's semantics. The carried session\n   818\tmust distinguish *where* the failure was: a **load** failure carries the\n   819\tcursor before the failing batch (it will be re-read); a **run/flush**\n   820\tfailure after a successful load carries the cursor *after* that batch and\n   821\tthe `:consumer` as the failing `run` left it (for the expander: its partially\n   822\tflushed slots), so a retry neither re-reads B nor re-appends what B already\n   823\tdelivered. This document depends on that fix and does not work around it.\n   824\t\n   825\t```\n   826\t  yang ──▶ program-in ──observe──▶ expander ──append──▶ program-out ──observe──▶ evaluator\n   827\t                                       │\n   828\t                                       └──append──▶ log (events, m-tagged output)\n   829\t```\n   830\t\n   831\tEvery evaluator observes `program-out` through its unchanged loader:\n   832\t`ast-walker/vm-load-program`, `linearize/loader ∘ semantic`, the stack\n   833\tcompiler. None composes anything macro-related.\n   834\t\n   835\t---\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
